(function(){'use strict';var lib=window.JASPER_FANFIC_DATA;if(!lib||!Array.isArray(lib.series))return;var edits={"after-always-14":{"number":14,"title":"Night Work","subtitle":"After Always · Chapter 14 of 40","content":"Snape taught the Patronus Charm like he taught everything else: as if failure were an irritating but unsurprising condition I had personally invented.\n\n“Again.”\n\nA thread of silver mist left my wand and collapsed.\n\n“Your focus wandered.”\n\n“I noticed.”\n\n“Then why did you permit it?”\n\n“Because thoughts are famously obedient.”\n\n“They can be trained.”\n\n“Not everyone stores theirs in labeled jars.”\n\nHis expression sharpened.\n\n“Do you imagine Occlumency is filing?”\n\n“I imagine yours has shelves.”\n\n“You imagine too much.”\n\n“Again?”\n\n“Again.”\n\nWe practiced in an unused classroom near the Astronomy Tower because Snape said the dungeons encouraged the wrong associations after the Dementor breach. I suspected he also preferred a room with windows.\n\nHe stood near the door, arms folded, black robes making him look like a disapproving shadow.\n\nI tried to find the memory I had chosen.\n\nNot my happiest.\n\nHe had corrected me on that immediately.\n\n“Happiness is vague,” he said. “Use something specific enough to resist substitution.”\n\nSo I chose a moment that belonged only to me: warm light, safety, the sensation of laughter arriving after I had been certain I had forgotten how.\n\nThe details did not need to be anyone else's.\n\nThey only needed to be mine.\n\nI raised my wand.\n\nSilver mist gathered.\n\nThis time it held shape for almost two seconds before dissolving.\n\nSnape's eyebrows rose.\n\n“Better.”\n\nI grinned.\n\nHis face immediately regretted encouraging me.\n\n“Do not become smug.”\n\n“You said better.”\n\n“I was describing a measurable improvement, not granting permission for celebration.”\n\n“Too late.”\n\nWe went again.\n\nBy midnight, my arm ached and Snape's voice had gone rough.\n\n“Enough.”\n\n“One more.”\n\n“No.”\n\n“You sound like Pomfrey.”\n\n“An unforgivable insult.”\n\n“One more.”\n\nHe studied me.\n\n“Why?”\n\nI lowered the wand.\n\nBecause I did not want to return to my room.\n\nBecause practice with him had become easier than sleep.\n\nBecause when I failed, he corrected technique instead of making the failure about my worth.\n\nBecause every time he said again, some stubborn part of me heard the message underneath it: I expect you can do it.\n\nI shrugged.\n\n“I'm close.”\n\nHe knew I was lying.\n\nNot about being close.\n\nAbout that being the whole reason.\n\nHe did not expose me.\n\n“One more,” he said.\n\nI raised my wand.\n\nThe silver shape that emerged was still indistinct. Four-legged, perhaps. Or winged. It moved once across the floor before breaking into light.\n\nI stared at the empty space.\n\nSnape said nothing.\n\nI looked at him.\n\nHe was smiling.\n\nBarely.\n\nBut enough.\n\n“I saw that.”\n\nHis expression vanished at once.\n\n“You saw nothing.”\n\n“My favorite word.”\n\n“Go to bed.”\n\n“You first.”\n\nWe ended up back in the Potions classroom instead.\n\nNight work had become a habit neither of us acknowledged. The castle was quieter after eleven. Builders left. McGonagall stopped sending notes. The portraits grew sleepy and less intrusive.\n\nWe brewed because brewing required exactness but not conversation.\n\nThat night we began a restorative draught for Snape's nerves.\n\nPomfrey had approved the formula with modifications. Snape had rejected two of them. I had restored one after proving his alternative increased nausea.\n\nHe had been insufferable about losing.\n\nI treasured it.\n\n“Cut the root thinner,” he said.\n\n“It will dissolve too quickly.”\n\n“Not at this temperature.”\n\n“The cauldron will rise by three degrees when we add the resin.”\n\n“Two.”\n\n“Three.”\n\nHe checked.\n\nThe needle climbed.\n\nThree degrees.\n\nSeverus stopped stirring and watched the gauge for a full ten seconds.\n\n“Your thermometer is calibrated correctly?” he asked.\n\n“I checked it before we started.”\n\n“Against what?”\n\n“The reference in your locked cabinet. You gave me the key.”\n\nThe expression he made suggested he had not intended trust to be so inconvenient.\n\nHe checked the reading himself, then adjusted the flame by the smallest movement of his wand. The potion changed from cloudy amber to a clear, rather dangerous-looking gold. He did not congratulate me. He wrote the revised temperature on the margin of the recipe in ink too dark to mistake for a pencil correction.\n\n“Keep the old value,” I said.\n\n“I am not an idiot.”\n\n“No, but sometimes you're a very proud scientist.”\n\n“Scientists are permitted pride when their instruments are not conspiring against them.”\n\nI braced my hands against the workbench and watched his left sleeve brush the edge of mine. I wanted to touch him. The wanting wasn't new; being able to admit it without imagining his reaction was the unfamiliar part.\n\n“Severus,” I said.\n\nHe kept his eyes on the potion. “If you intend to ask for the reference key again, the answer is no.”\n\n“I wasn't.”\n\n“Then I shall be grateful for the surprise.”\n\nI turned my hand palm up beside the recipe. He looked down at it, then at me. He had ink on one finger and a burn mark on the cuff of his robe. Eventually he put two fingertips against my wrist.\n\n“Your pulse is rather fast,” he observed.\n\n“It would be worse if you started taking notes.”\n\n“A tempting prospect.”\n\nThe corner of his mouth lifted. He removed his hand only when the timer sounded. I was absurdly disappointed, which he noticed and chose, for once, not to exploit.\n\n“After we decant this,” he said, “you may ask me a question that is not about the potion.”\n\nI stared. He returned to his cauldron, looking intolerably pleased with himself.\n\nI smiled.\n\nSnape stared at the thermometer as if betrayal had become measurable.\n\n“Do not.”\n\n“I haven't said anything.”\n\n“Your face is shouting.”\n\n“Three.”\n\n“I can read.”\n\n“Can you?”\n\nHe flicked a drop of water at me with his wand.\n\nI stared.\n\nHe stared back.\n\n“Did you just splash me?”\n\n“No.”\n\n“My sleeve is wet.”\n\n“Condensation.”\n\n“There is no condensation.”\n\n“Then your technique has produced an unprecedented meteorological event.”\n\nI laughed so hard I nearly dropped the knife.\n\nHe looked pleased with himself.\n\nThat was worse.\n\n“You're impossible.”\n\n“I have been told.”\n\nThe potion simmered.\n\nSnape took the stirring rod. His injured hand was steadier now. Not perfect. Better.\n\nI watched the movement of his fingers.\n\nHe noticed.\n\n“Problem?”\n\n“No.”\n\nHis eyes narrowed.\n\nI could have lied.\n\nInstead I said, “Your hand is better.”\n\nHe looked down.\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Does it feel better?”\n\n“Some days.”\n\n“Today?”\n\nA pause.\n\n“Yes.”\n\nI smiled.\n\nHe did not tell me not to.\n\nThe potion needed forty minutes at a low simmer.\n\nWe sat on opposite sides of the central table.\n\nSnape opened one of the journals from Spinner's End.\n\nI read a book on curse contamination.\n\nThe quiet settled around us.\n\nThis was different from the silence on the staircase. Different from the wary quiet after an argument.\n\nComfortable silence, I realized.\n\nThe thought startled me enough that I turned a page too loudly.\n\nSnape looked up.\n\n“What?”\n\n“Nothing.”\n\nHis expression said liar.\n\nI looked back at the book.\n\nA few minutes later he said, “You do not need to remain until the potion is finished.”\n\n“I know.”\n\n“You are tired.”\n\n“So are you.”\n\n“I am accustomed to it.”\n\n“That doesn't make it healthy.”\n\n“No.”\n\nI glanced up.\n\nHe had admitted that too easily.\n\n“You're getting reasonable.”\n\n“Do not insult me twice in one evening.”\n\nI smiled.\n\nAt one in the morning, the potion reached the proper color: smoky violet with a silver ring at the edge.\n\nSnape lifted a sample.\n\n“Acceptable.”\n\n“High praise.”\n\n“It may not kill me.”\n\n“Glowing praise.”\n\nHe poured six doses into dark glass bottles.\n\nOne slipped slightly in his fingers.\n\nI reached automatically.\n\nSo did he.\n\nOur hands closed around the same bottle.\n\nWe stopped.\n\nHis fingers lay over mine.\n\nNo gloves.\n\nWarm skin.\n\nThe contact lasted one second too long.\n\nThen two.\n\nSnape's eyes lifted to my face.\n\nEverything in the room became too precise.\n\nThe flame beneath the cauldron.\n\nThe smell of resin.\n\nThe rough edge of his scar above the collar.\n\nMy own pulse, suddenly ridiculous.\n\nHe let go first.\n\n“Secure the stopper,” he said.\n\nHis voice had dropped.\n\nI did.\n\nNeither of us mentioned it.\n\nWe cleaned the laboratory with unnatural attention.\n\nAt the door, I said, “Patronus practice tomorrow?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“After the audit?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Night work?”\n\nHe looked at me.\n\n“That depends.”\n\n“On what?”\n\n“Whether you intend to continue producing weather indoors.”\n\nI laughed.\n\nHe opened the door for me.\n\nThe gesture was small.\n\nAlmost formal.\n\nI passed him into the corridor.\n\n“Good night, Snape.”\n\n“Good night.”\n\nI walked away.\n\nAt the first turn, I looked back.\n\nHe was still standing in the doorway.\n\nWhen our eyes met, he retreated inside and closed it.\n\nI smiled all the way upstairs.\n\nThe night work became a pattern before either of us admitted it was one.\n\nI would finish the official audit, eat something recognizable as dinner, and return to the dungeon with the intention of staying for twenty minutes. Severus would claim not to have expected me. A second mug would already be on the desk.\n\nOn the fourth night, I held it up.\n\n“Coincidence?”\n\n“Preparedness.”\n\n“For exactly the drink I take?”\n\n“You are tediously predictable.”\n\n“Sugar?”\n\nHe shoved the bowl toward me without looking.\n\nWe spent an hour comparing damaged inventory ledgers. The work should have been dull. Instead it developed its own rhythm: my questions, his clipped answers, the scratch of quills, the occasional insult when one of us made an arithmetic error.\n\nNear midnight his left hand began to tremble.\n\nHe tucked it beneath the desk.\n\nI kept reading.\n\nFive minutes later I said, “We're done.”\n\n“We are not.”\n\n“You've reread the same figure three times.”\n\nHis expression darkened.\n\n“I'm not ordering you to rest,” I said. “I'm announcing that my own brain is soup and I refuse to let you benefit from it.”\n\nThat gave him a route out that did not require admitting weakness.\n\nHe saw exactly what I had done.\n\n“You are manipulative.”\n\n“Learning from an expert.”\n\nHe closed the ledger.\n\nThe next night, he was the one who ended work first.\n\nThat was definitely a problem.","word_count":1656},"after-always-17":{"number":17,"title":"The Language of Hands","subtitle":"After Always · Chapter 17 of 40","content":"Snape's right hand improved before his left.\n\nI knew because he began stirring clockwise again without switching grip halfway through.\n\nHe knew I knew because he caught me watching.\n\n“Your fascination with my extremities is becoming conspicuous.”\n\nI nearly dropped the measuring spoon.\n\nHe looked at me.\n\nI looked at him.\n\nThen, unbelievably, color rose along the tops of his cheekbones.\n\nI started laughing.\n\n“Do not.”\n\n“You said it.”\n\n“I did not mean—”\n\n“That makes it better.”\n\nHis expression became murderous.\n\nI leaned against the table, helpless.\n\n“Are you twelve?” he asked.\n\n“Currently, yes.”\n\n“You are impossible.”\n\n“So I've been told.”\n\nHis own line.\n\nThat only made me laugh harder.\n\nHe turned back to the cauldron with the stiff dignity of a man betrayed by language.\n\nWe were brewing a complex nerve restorative for St Mungo's, not for him this time. The hospital had heard about our audit work and sent a request for help reproducing a pre-war formulation whose maker had died. Snape knew the recipe. I knew the modern stabilizers. Between us, we had spent three days arguing it into something better.\n\nThe potion required paired work during the final stage.\n\nOne person had to stir continuously.\n\nSeverus set the timer beside the cauldron and turned his injured hand out of view.\n\n\"You can tell me if your grip hurts,\" I said.\n\n\"You have a talent for announcing the obvious.\"\n\n\"Pain isn't an examination you have to pass.\"\n\nHe looked sharply at me. \"Pain is information, Jasper. It is not an accomplishment.\"\n\n\"Then what information is it giving you?\"\n\nThe timer clicked through several seconds. Finally he pushed the recipe toward me.\n\n\"If the liquid clouds, reduce the heat. Do not waste silverleaf correcting a mistake that has not occurred.\"\n\nI took the stirrer. He watched my hand without reaching to reposition it.\n\n\"You can correct me,\" I said. \"Use words first.\"\n\nHis ears colored. \"I thought we were discussing laboratory protocol.\"\n\n\"We are.\"\n\n\"Then cease making it sound flirtatious.\"\n\n\"You're the one blushing.\"\n\nHis response was a withering look and the careful movement of the timer to my side of the bench. I treated that little concession with more respect than any public declaration.\n\nWhen the steam cleared, he said, very quietly, \"Thank you for asking.\"\n\nHe didn't specify which question. I let him keep the answer.\n\nThe other had to add twelve ingredients at exact intervals, some no more than four seconds apart.\n\nSnape took the stirring rod.\n\nI prepared the tray.\n\n“Ready?” I asked.\n\n“I was ready six minutes ago.”\n\n“You were complaining six minutes ago.”\n\n“I can do both.”\n\n“I've noticed.”\n\nWe began.\n\nPorcupine quill.\n\nThree turns.\n\nSilverleaf.\n\nSeven turns.\n\nA pinch of crushed moonstone.\n\nSnape's hand moved with elegant precision.\n\nI watched the potion color change from amber to pearl.\n\n“Now,” he said.\n\nI added salamander blood.\n\nThe surface flashed green.\n\n“Too fast.”\n\n“It was on time.”\n\n“Half a second early.”\n\n“You cannot possibly know that.”\n\n“I can count.”\n\n“So can I.”\n\n“Debatable.”\n\n“Keep stirring.”\n\nHis mouth twitched.\n\nThe next addition required me to reach across him.\n\nI warned him first.\n\n“Left side.”\n\nHe shifted.\n\nMy forearm brushed his wrist.\n\nNo one froze.\n\nThat was new.\n\nWe continued.\n\nAt ingredient nine, his left hand began to tremble where it steadied the cauldron handle.\n\nHe ignored it.\n\nAt ingredient ten, the tremor worsened.\n\nI saw his jaw tighten.\n\n“Switch,” I said.\n\n“No.”\n\n“Your hand.”\n\n“I am aware.”\n\n“Then switch.”\n\n“We lose the rhythm.”\n\n“I can take it.”\n\n“You have not practiced this sequence.”\n\n“I watched you.”\n\n“That is not equivalent.”\n\nThe potion darkened at the edge.\n\nWe had seconds.\n\nI reached for the stirring rod.\n\nSnape's hand closed over mine.\n\n“Wait.”\n\nHis grip was firm despite the tremor.\n\n“On my count,” he said.\n\nI understood.\n\nWe could transfer without breaking rhythm if our hands overlapped for a full turn.\n\n“One.”\n\nHis fingers tightened around mine on the rod.\n\n“Two.”\n\nWe stirred together.\n\n“Three.”\n\nHe released.\n\nI took over.\n\n“Counterclockwise now,” he said.\n\nI obeyed.\n\nHe added ingredient eleven with his right hand.\n\nThen twelve.\n\nThe potion flashed white.\n\nPerfect.\n\nWe both exhaled.\n\nMy hand remained on the stirring rod.\n\nHis hovered close.\n\nThe moment should have ended there.\n\nInstead he said, “Your grip was acceptable.”\n\nI looked at him.\n\n“Acceptable?”\n\n“Do not become emotional.”\n\n“I'm overwhelmed.”\n\n“Control yourself.”\n\n“I may need a chair.”\n\nHis mouth curved.\n\nThe teasing felt different after our hands had moved together.\n\nLess like defense.\n\nMore like play.\n\nThat frightened me more than sarcasm ever had.\n\nThe fourth ingredient was late by two seconds.\n\n“Stop,” Severus said.\n\nI froze with the little glass measure in my hand. The potion's surface had gone from blue to a cloudy green at the edges. My first instinct was to add more stabilizer, to prove I could rescue the work before he lost patience.\n\nHe caught the movement. “If you compensate without measuring, we shall have an expensive bottle of speculation.”\n\nI put the measure down.\n\nHe tested a sample in a clear dish, then compared it with the reference card we'd written that morning. The result wasn't dangerous, but it wasn't the formulation St Mungo's had requested. We could keep the batch for practice. We couldn't send it for a patient.\n\n“That's three hours,” I said.\n\n“Four, if you count my increasingly regrettable company.”\n\nI looked at his left hand. His grip on the rod was tight enough to whiten his knuckles, though he hadn't complained once.\n\n“You didn't tell me it was hurting.”\n\n“It was not relevant until it became relevant.”\n\n“That's a lovely definition. Completely useless.”\n\nHe glared at me. Then he released the rod and rolled his fingers open. There were faint red marks where the handle had pressed against his skin.\n\nWe took a break that cost us the batch but saved the rest of the afternoon. I fitted a thicker grip to the stirring rod, and he tested it without ceremony. He would not have thanked me for pity. He did, however, tell me exactly where the grip needed reshaping.\n\nWhen we began again, we assigned the timing aloud. He called the intervals; I counted the additions; neither pretended a mistake belonged to only one pair of hands. By the final stage, the surface stayed clear.\n\n“Acceptable,” Severus said.\n\n“From you, that's practically a serenade.”\n\n“Then your musical standards are as appalling as your arithmetic.”\n\nHe checked the vial seal himself, but he let me sign the production record beside him.\n\nWe bottled the potion.\n\nLater, while cleaning, I cut my thumb on a chipped vial.\n\nIt was shallow.\n\nI hissed.\n\nSnape turned immediately.\n\n“Show me.”\n\n“It's nothing.”\n\nHis eyebrow rose.\n\nI smiled.\n\n“Right. I hear it now.”\n\n“Your hypocrisy is refreshing.”\n\nI held out my hand.\n\nSnape took it.\n\nNot my wrist.\n\nMy hand.\n\nHis fingers supported my palm while he examined the cut.\n\nThe contact was practical.\n\nIt still changed my breathing.\n\nHe noticed.\n\nOf course he did.\n\nHis eyes lifted briefly to mine, then back to the injury.\n\n“Stay still.”\n\n“I am.”\n\n“You moved.”\n\n“I breathed.”\n\n“Unnecessarily dramatic.”\n\nHe cleaned the cut with his wand and applied a drop of dittany.\n\nThe sting made me twitch.\n\nHis thumb pressed lightly against the base of mine to steady me.\n\nWarm.\n\nCareful.\n\nHis hands were not soft. There were calluses along his fingers from years of knives, quills, cauldrons, wands.\n\nI had noticed them before.\n\nNot like this.\n\nThe cut sealed.\n\nHe did not let go immediately.\n\nNeither did I pull away.\n\nHis gaze returned to my face.\n\nThe room narrowed.\n\nI thought he might say something sharp to break it.\n\nHe did not.\n\nInstead his thumb moved once across my palm.\n\nBarely.\n\nAn accident, perhaps.\n\nExcept Severus Snape did very little by accident when he was watching himself.\n\nMy pulse kicked.\n\nHe released me.\n\n“There.”\n\n“Thank you.”\n\nHe turned to the sink.\n\nI stared at my healed thumb.\n\nRidiculous.\n\nI was an adult. A touch was a touch. A healer might have done the same. A colleague might have.\n\nBut Snape had rules about contact.\n\nSo did I.\n\nWe had spent weeks teaching each other that touch required intention or necessity.\n\nThe cut had been necessary.\n\nThe extra second had not.\n\nNeither had the thumb across my palm.\n\nI looked at his back.\n\n“Snape.”\n\nHis shoulders tensed.\n\n“Yes?”\n\nI could ask.\n\nI could name it.\n\nI could force the moment into language and watch him retreat behind every wall he owned.\n\nSo I chose something else.\n\n“Your left hand did well today.”\n\nHe was quiet.\n\n“Better,” he said.\n\n“Yes.”\n\nHe turned.\n\nThere was gratitude in his face and something warmer beneath it.\n\n“Yours,” he said, “was adequate.”\n\nI smiled.\n\n“Careful. Compliments twice in one day.”\n\n“A temporary lapse.”\n\n“I'll document it.”\n\n“I will destroy the record.”\n\n“Then I'll make copies.”\n\nHis eyes narrowed with familiar amusement.\n\nWe cleaned the rest of the laboratory side by side.\n\nAt the end of the night, he handed me my coat.\n\nOur fingers touched.\n\nThe change was most obvious when I stopped looking for it.\n\nA month earlier, every accidental touch had become an event. A brush of fingers over a jar. His hand at my elbow on the stairs. Mine hovering near his back when his balance failed but refusing to land without permission.\n\nNow touch had begun to acquire grammar.\n\nA palm held out meant help me up.\n\nTwo fingers against my wrist meant stop before you ruin that measurement.\n\nThe back of his knuckles against my sleeve meant move; I am carrying something dangerous and apparently words are beneath me.\n\nOne evening I found him struggling with a stopper that had sealed itself too tightly. His left hand was trembling.\n\n“Want me?” I asked.\n\nHe froze.\n\nI realized how the question sounded at exactly the same moment he did.\n\nHeat climbed my face.\n\n“The jar,” I said.\n\n“I understood.”\n\n“You absolutely did not.”\n\nHis mouth curved with slow cruelty. “Your phrasing was imprecise.”\n\nI snatched the jar from him, opened it, and shoved it back.\n\n“Fixed.”\n\n“Remarkably efficient.”\n\n“Say one more thing.”\n\nHe looked directly at me. “Want me?”\n\nI nearly dropped the stopper.\n\nHis laugh rasped out before he could stop it.\n\nThat night, the language of hands gained sarcasm too.\n\nThis time neither of us pretended it was accidental.","word_count":1724},"after-always-32":{"number":32,"title":"What Lily Was and Was Not","subtitle":"After Always · Chapter 32 of 40","content":"The first time I asked Snape whether he had been in love with Lily or in love with what she represented, he nearly choked on tea.\n\nWe were at Spinner's End, sitting on the floor because the terrible chair had finally collapsed under a repair charm and Snape was taking this as a personal insult.\n\nHe set down his cup.\n\n“What an appalling question.”\n\n“Too personal?”\n\n“Too imprecise.”\n\nI smiled.\n\n“That sounds promising.”\n\n“It should not.”\n\nI had not planned to ask.\n\nThe question had been circling since the hearing, sharpened by newspapers that kept reducing Lily to a symbol in Snape's story and Snape to a symbol in hers.\n\nI did not want to do the same.\n\n“You can refuse,” I said.\n\n“I am aware.”\n\nHe leaned back against the wall.\n\nThe sitting room smelled of dust and fresh plaster. One of his laboratory plans lay open on the floor between us.\n\n“At eleven,” he said, “I loved her because she was kind to me.”\n\nI stayed quiet.\n\n“At fifteen, I loved her because she was clever and stubborn and because she had become part of how I imagined my own future.”\n\nHis face tightened.\n\n“At sixteen, I loved her badly.”\n\nThe words were simple.\n\n“What does that mean?”\n\n“I wanted her understanding without giving sufficient respect to what she objected to. I resented people she cared for because I believed my history with her should matter more than her choices.”\n\nJames.\n\nHer friends.\n\nThe people Snape had hated or dismissed.\n\n“And later?”\n\n“Later I loved someone who was dead.”\n\nHis eyes moved toward the window.\n\n“That makes honesty difficult.”\n\n“Because she couldn't change?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\nThe answer surprised me with its clarity.\n\n“Memory preserves what it preserves. Grief edits. Regret edits more.”\n\nI thought of the girl in the Pensieve. Laughing. Angry. Defending her sister. Refusing his apology when it was not enough.\n\n“She wasn't a saint,” I said.\n\nSnape's eyes flashed.\n\n“No.”\n\nThe force of agreement made me smile.\n\n“What?”\n\n“You sounded offended on her behalf.”\n\n“I am offended by most simplifications.”\n\n“Consistent.”\n\nHe looked down at his tea.\n\n“Lily could be impatient. Judgmental. Reckless when angry. She enjoyed being right more than she admitted.”\n\n“You liked that.”\n\n“Sometimes.”\n\n“You hated it when it was aimed at you.”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Also consistent.”\n\nHis mouth twitched.\n\nI shifted closer, our shoulders touching against the wall.\n\n“Do you think she would forgive you?”\n\nSnape became very still.\n\nThe reporter had asked that.\n\nI regretted the question immediately.\n\n“Sorry.”\n\n“No.”\n\nI looked at him.\n\nHe was not refusing.\n\nHe was answering.\n\n“No?”\n\n“I do not know,” he said. “And I no longer believe I am entitled to construct an answer for her.”\n\nThat felt important.\n\n“For years I imagined what she would say because there was no one to contradict me.”\n\nHis expression tightened.\n\n“That is not conversation. It is ventriloquism.”\n\nI let the words sit.\n\n“So you stopped?”\n\n“I am trying to.”\n\nI took his hand.\n\nHe looked at our fingers.\n\n“Does being with me feel disloyal to her?” I asked.\n\nHis head snapped toward me.\n\n“No.”\n\nImmediate.\n\nMy chest loosened.\n\n“Did it ever?”\n\nA pause.\n\n“Yes.”\n\nI appreciated the truth more than a prettier answer.\n\n“When?”\n\n“The first time I wanted to kiss you.”\n\n“In Hogsmeade?”\n\n“Earlier.”\n\nI blinked.\n\n“How much earlier?”\n\nHe looked annoyed.\n\n“Why is that relevant?”\n\n“Severus.”\n\nThe name slipped out naturally.\n\nWe both froze.\n\nI had called him Snape almost from the beginning. He had called me whatever the moment allowed: Consultant, occasionally a ridiculous nickname based on some professional error, more often nothing at all because names were unnecessary in a room built for two.\n\nHis first name felt intimate in a way kissing had not.\n\n“Again,” he said quietly.\n\nMy pulse jumped.\n\n“Severus.”\n\nHis eyes closed for half a second.\n\nWhen they opened, he looked younger and older at once.\n\n“The first time,” he said, “was the night you caught the memory vial.”\n\nI stared.\n\n“That early?”\n\n“I did not say I wanted to act on it.”\n\n“You wanted to kiss me.”\n\n“I considered the possibility.”\n\n“That is Snape for wanted to kiss me.”\n\n“Do not become unbearable.”\n\n“Too late.”\n\nI was grinning.\n\nHe looked deeply irritated and slightly embarrassed.\n\nIt was wonderful.\n\nThen his expression sobered.\n\n“I felt disloyal because desire seemed like movement.”\n\n“Away from her?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“And now?”\n\n“I understand movement is not replacement.”\n\nI squeezed his hand.\n\n“Good.”\n\nHis gaze sharpened.\n\n“You were worried.”\n\n“Of course I was.”\n\n“You said neither of us was required to compete with the dead.”\n\n“I meant it. That doesn't mean I'm incapable of insecurity.”\n\nHe studied me.\n\n“What were you afraid of?”\n\nThat question was harder to answer.\n\nI looked at the cracked plaster on the opposite wall.\n\n“That I would become evidence that you were healed.”\n\nSnape's expression changed.\n\n“Explain.”\n\n“That people would look at us and say, see, he moved on, so everything is fine now. Or that you'd look at us that way.”\n\nHis face hardened with something like offense.\n\n“I would never use you that way.”\n\n“I know that now.”\n\n“And before?”\n\n“I didn't know anything before.”\n\nA fair point.\n\nHe looked down.\n\nA knock came from the front room. The builder had left a revised sketch under the door and gone, apparently aware that Severus would prefer not to be interrupted while deciding whether a chair could be accused of attempted murder.\n\nSeverus retrieved the sketch but did not open it. He stood with the paper in his hand.\n\n\"People have spent years trying to make Lily's death into a neat account of my life,\" he said. \"It was never neat.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"You didn't know her.\"\n\n\"No. And that means there's a limit to what I can claim about her.\"\n\nHe considered that. I knew his history only through what he'd chosen to tell me and what had been recorded by others, some of it badly. It would have been easy to imagine that accepting his memories gave me ownership over her. It didn't.\n\n\"I don't need to be your only love,\" I said. \"You know I'm ethically non-monogamous and prefer polyamory. But that doesn't mean I have the right to sort your feelings into a system that makes sense to me. And I don't want you to use Lily as evidence that you're incapable of choosing a future.\"\n\nSeverus leaned against the doorway. \"You are remarkably determined to deprive me of a good excuse.\"\n\n\"Only the ones that make you miserable.\"\n\n\"That is a broad category.\"\n\nThe joke didn't solve anything. It gave us room to stay beside the difficulty without treating it as a test we had to pass before being affectionate. He opened the builder's sketch, frowned, and handed me the list of repairs that could wait.\n\nI took the pencil and wrote down a time for the next inspection. He didn't tell me the house was healed. He asked if I would like to come back with him, and I said yes, as long as we had time to do something besides repair walls.\n\n“You are not proof of recovery,” he said.\n\nThe sentence warmed me unexpectedly.\n\n“You are not a cure. You are not a reward. You are not a substitute.”\n\nEach word precise.\n\n“What am I?”\n\nHis mouth tightened.\n\n“A person asking irritating questions on my sitting-room floor.”\n\nI laughed.\n\nThen he added, “Whom I love.”\n\nEverything stopped.\n\nNo build-up.\n\nNo speech.\n\nHe looked as startled as I felt.\n\n“Severus.”\n\n“Do not.”\n\n“You just—”\n\n“I am aware.”\n\n“You said—”\n\n“I am aware.”\n\nHis face had gone red.\n\nI was smiling so hard it hurt.\n\n“This is unbearable,” he muttered.\n\n“I love you too.”\n\nSilence.\n\nHis eyes snapped to mine.\n\nThe room changed.\n\nNot because love was new.\n\nBecause language had caught up.\n\nSnape looked down at our hands.\n\n“Are you certain?”\n\n“No.”\n\nHis face fell.\n\nI squeezed his fingers.\n\n“I mean I can't prove it in a laboratory. I can't tell you the exact day it started. I can't promise what we'll be in ten years.”\n\nI leaned closer.\n\n“But yes. I love you.”\n\nHis expression cracked into a small, helpless smile.\n\n“Pedantic,” he said.\n\n“You taught me.”\n\n“I regret everything.”\n\n“Liar.”\n\nHe kissed me.\n\nSlowly.\n\nThe laboratory plans crumpled under my knee.\n\nNeither of us cared.\n\nLater, we smoothed them out on the table.\n\nOne corner of the parchment had taken a crease that refused to lie flat. Severus tried a restoration charm, watched the paper curl in the opposite direction, and muttered something spectacularly rude about the limits of contemporary spellcraft.\n\n“It's a workbench plan,” I said. “Not an international treaty.”\n\n“Both are improved by accurate measurements.”\n\nHe fetched a weight from the laboratory and set it over the fold. Beneath his hand the drawing showed a narrow shelf, two ventilation outlets and the safer corridor we'd argued about for days. The room would be somewhere we could work without standing in each other's way. I wanted that more than another grand gesture.\n\n“You left space beside the sink,” I said.\n\n“For the additional wash station.”\n\n“You mean the one I asked for?”\n\n“I have been known to listen.”\n\n“Rarely without a complaint.”\n\n“I reserve the right to object to stupid suggestions. This was not one.”\n\nI studied him in the lamplight. His hair had fallen loose around his face. I reached up to tuck it back, stopping before I touched him. He saw the movement and leaned toward my hand just enough to answer.\n\nI brushed the dark strand behind his ear. The warmth there startled me; he drew a sharp breath and caught my wrist, not to push it away.\n\n“Do not look so delighted,” he said.\n\n“I'm trying not to.”\n\n“You are failing.”\n\nHe kissed my knuckles and released my hand so that he could move the weight to the other end of the page. Neither of us mentioned Lily. Not because she had become unimportant, but because the afternoon had room for a new room, a crooked plan, and the simple physical fact that Severus had wanted me near him. I tucked the pencil beside the drawing and stayed there while he measured the shelf again.\n\nLily remained part of his life.\n\nSo did I.\n\nThose facts did not need to destroy each other.\n\nBoth could be true.","word_count":1722},"after-always-42":{"number":42,"title":"Not an Appointment","subtitle":"The Lanternweald — expansion pass two","content":"Iroh invited Severus Snape to tea by sending him a formal card.\n\nIt was cream-colored, folded once, and bore a small green dragon stamped in the corner. The handwriting inside was beautiful.\n\nDear Professor Snape, it said. Your company would be welcome at the Jasmine Steps tasting on Thursday. No speeches are required.\n\nSeverus read the card twice.\n\nThen he put it facedown on the kitchen table.\n\n\"Absolutely not.\"\n\nI was cutting an apple into pieces small enough to eat while sorting a stack of invoices. I did not look up.\n\n\"Are you declining the tea or the lack of speeches?\"\n\n\"The implication.\"\n\n\"Which implication?\"\n\n\"That I have become a public exhibit.\"\n\nI put down the apple knife. Severus had not discarded Iroh's card. For all his contempt of social obligations, he had placed it where he could see the green dragon on the fold.\n\n\"You can decline,\" I said. \"He won't demand an explanation.\"\n\n\"I am aware how an invitation works.\"\n\n\"Then why are you glaring at it?\"\n\nHe rested a finger on the handwriting. \"Because I recognize someone accustomed to making a room comfortable even when the room has no right to ask it of him.\"\n\nThat caught me. \"You think Iroh is performing?\"\n\n\"I think he knows exactly when to be charming and when to stop. That is not the same thing.\"\n\nI remembered the former general's voice turning hard when a real decision needed to be made. Severus had never been there. He had noticed the habit without pretending to share the man's history.\n\n\"Talk about tea,\" I suggested.\n\n\"Tea has soluble compounds. At least we could discuss something measurable.\"\n\n\"And if you enjoy the visit?\"\n\n\"I shall conceal the scandal.\"\n\nHe moved the card an inch nearer. It was not yet a yes, and I did not steal one from his silence.\n\nThat evening he asked whether steeping temperature altered the bitterness of jasmine. I answered the actual question. His interest could belong to him without becoming my victory.\n\nI set down the knife.\n\n\"He's inviting you to drink tea.\"\n\n\"He is inviting the notorious surviving spy of the war into a room full of people who think an interesting guest constitutes an evening's entertainment.\"\n\nI folded the card and looked at the back. Nothing there suggested a lecture, a public question period, or a dramatic unveiling of Severus's biography.\n\n\"You could just say no,\" I said.\n\n\"I did.\"\n\n\"Then I'll tell him.\"\n\nSeverus turned toward me. He seemed almost disappointed by how easily I'd accepted his refusal.\n\n\"You intend to attend?\"\n\n\"Yes. I like tea.\"\n\n\"An insufficient reason to subject yourself to crowds.\"\n\n\"I also like Iroh.\"\n\nA small movement at the corner of his jaw told me he'd heard the choice of words. I gave him time to decide whether he wanted to ask the question behind it.\n\nHe did not.\n\nI went back to my apple.\n\nThat was not the end of the argument.\n\nIt followed us through breakfast, into the annex, and all the way to Moonroot Archive. Severus insisted he was not offended. He was merely unimpressed by the presumption of strangers who thought they were entitled to his time.\n\nI agreed that strangers were not entitled to his time.\n\nHe seemed to find this aggravating.\n\nNear noon, Iroh arrived in the archive carrying a basket of clean cups for the pantry. He greeted Neri, complimented Pip on a repaired door latch, and came toward our worktable with a smile.\n\nIroh set the basket down and checked Mina's loan sheet. The count matched. He had brought back every cup that the pantry had agreed to lend.\n\nSeverus watched him with the suspicion he usually reserved for mislabeled ingredients.\n\n\"Are you delivering crockery or an argument?\" he asked.\n\n\"Only the crockery, Professor. The argument would require a separate appointment.\"\n\nI nearly laughed. Iroh's expression was pleasant, but not empty; he had the quick eyes of someone who had once made plans under much worse conditions than a crowded archive.\n\nNeri signed for the cups. Iroh thanked them and folded the receipt. He didn't ask to see the historical records or make a claim on the workspace because he'd brought refreshments.\n\nSeverus tapped his pen against the table. \"I presume this is not a surprise inspection.\"\n\n\"Not unless you have been brewing tea without a permit.\"\n\n\"There is no such permit.\"\n\n\"Excellent. Then we have avoided another form.\"\n\nMina's numbers remained on the cloth, and the cups went to their owners. Even a small gift or invitation, I thought, could arrive with its proper limits intact.\n\n\"I hope the card reached you,\" he said.\n\n\"It did,\" Severus answered.\n\n\"Good.\"\n\n\"I shall not be attending.\"\n\nIroh bowed his head slightly.\n\n\"Then I hope we may meet another day.\"\n\nSeverus looked as though someone had misplaced the rest of his argument.\n\n\"That is all?\"\n\n\"Would you prefer a lengthy attempt to change your mind?\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"Then I shall spare us both.\"\n\nIroh turned to me.\n\n\"I will save a cup for anyone who arrives before the kettle cools. If you have time, Jasper, you are welcome.\"\n\n\"I'll be there.\"\n\n\"Wonderful.\"\n\nHe left the basket behind and went to help Mara set up the afternoon pantry shelves.\n\nSeverus stared after him.\n\n\"He is either genuinely polite or very skilled at appearing so.\"\n\n\"Both could be true.\"\n\n\"I dislike uncertainty.\"\n\n\"I've noticed.\"\n\nThe rest of the day was ordinary in ways that became important only later. We cataloged three damaged journals. Severus corrected a mislabeled vial and did not insult the apprentice who had made the error. Neri brought a photograph of the underside of the well's old stone arch, and we compared it with the drawing from the washed-up ledger fragment.\n\nOne line on the old map was blank.\n\nSeverus marked it, but did not guess what it meant.\n\nIroh returned for the basket he'd left behind just as Neri brought a second copy of the washed-up ledger map.\n\n“Forgive the interruption,” he said. “Mara has discovered that an empty basket refuses to carry anything.”\n\nSeverus glanced at the basket. “A revelation for the ages.”\n\n“I thought so.”\n\nNeri spread the copy across the table. The blank line stopped just short of a dark mark near the lower maintenance passage. It might have been an erased signature. It might have been a damaged route label. A scholar had written in the margin that the mark showed where an old keeper had secretly opened the passage.\n\n“That is unsupported,” Severus said immediately.\n\nIroh leaned closer, hands behind his back. “If it were a route marker, would it not be drawn toward a place someone expected to reach?”\n\n“Only if we knew the mapmaker's notation. We don't.”\n\n“And if it were a signature?”\n\n“Then assigning an identity would be equally foolish.”\n\nI waited for Iroh to make an elegant pronouncement. Instead he studied the faint ink and frowned as though he were examining a battle map he no longer trusted.\n\n“In the war,” he said, “I have seen a general turn one uncertain line into a road because men were eager to march.”\n\nSeverus's expression changed. “A surprisingly rigorous observation.”\n\n“Do not encourage him,” I told Severus. “He'll want to illustrate it with tea leaves.”\n\n“I assure you,” Severus replied, “I require a specimen with a more predictable chemical structure.”\n\nNeri raised one hand.\n\n“Both of you. The ledger belongs to people who were denied the right to travel. I'd prefer our uncertainty be recorded before two clever men turn it into a contest.”\n\nThey were right. Severus made the annotation; Iroh signed as a reader who could confirm only what was visible on the copy. Neither claimed he knew the missing name. The original stayed protected, the lower passage stayed closed, and Neri decided which notes were suitable for public display.\n\nIroh collected his basket. “You would be welcome for tea another time, Professor.”\n\n“And you,” Severus answered, “would be welcome to examine a properly labeled map.”\n\nI nearly laughed. Neither of them seemed to realize that was their version of a friendly invitation.\n\nAt four o'clock, he closed his notebook.\n\n\"You should leave if you intend to be on time.\"\n\n\"Are you dismissing me?\"\n\n\"I am observing the clock.\"\n\n\"That is how you dismissed me yesterday.\"\n\n\"Then you have learned to recognize a pattern.\"\n\nI collected my bag and paused by the door.\n\n\"You could still come,\" I said. \"Not because anyone expects you to be charming. Just because you might like the tea.\"\n\nHe looked at me for a long moment.\n\n\"I would not like the people.\"\n\n\"Then enjoy your evening without them.\"\n\nHis expression softened slightly.\n\n\"Do not let them make you stay beyond your wishes.\"\n\n\"I won't.\"\n\nI went alone.\n\nJasmine Steps was crowded with the gentle noise of people who were not trying to impress one another. Greg had brought a guitar but was sitting with it unplayed across his knees. Katara was helping a neighbor carry a tray; Toph had occupied the chair with the best view of the garden despite not being interested in views.\n\nIroh poured a cup for me.\n\n\"Severus sends his regrets,\" I said.\n\n\"Does he?\"\n\n\"More accurately, he sends an exceptionally firm no.\"\n\n\"Then I am glad he was able to give it.\"\n\nWe drank the tea. It tasted of mountain mint and something warm beneath it that I could not name.\n\nAfter a while, Iroh asked how the archive was doing.\n\nI told him about the mislabeled recipe, the corrected copy, and Neri's old drainage diagrams. I did not tell him which pages Severus had kept private. It felt good to speak about our work without opening the parts of it that did not belong to me.\n\nGreg played a short melody after the cups were cleared. Toph complained that one note was half a beat late.\n\n\"That was on purpose,\" Greg said.\n\n\"Then you made a mistake intentionally. Congratulations.\"\n\nAang laughed so hard he nearly dropped his cup.\n\nI stayed until the lanterns came on, then walked back toward Moonroot alone. I could have stayed longer. I could have gone to another person's door. My relationships did not turn every independent evening into evidence for or against anybody. I wanted to go home.\n\nSeverus was in the kitchen at Spinner's End when I arrived.\n\nThe kettle had already been filled.\n\n\"How was the exhibition?\" he asked.\n\n\"Very disappointing. No one asked me to juggle, and nobody tried to turn me into a display.\"\n\n\"A shame.\"\n\n\"Greg played music.\"\n\n\"Predictable.\"\n\n\"Toph criticized the rhythm.\"\n\n\"An entirely reasonable response.\"\n\nI hung up my coat.\n\n\"Did you have a good evening?\"\n\n\"Quiet.\"\n\n\"Good.\"\n\nHe looked at me.\n\n\"That is all you intend to ask?\"\n\n\"Did you want me to make an argument for attending next time?\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"Then I won't.\"\n\nSeverus set two mugs on the table. He hesitated before speaking.\n\n\"I considered that you might interpret my refusal as disinterest in the people you care about.\"\n\n\"I didn't.\"\n\n\"Or as a demand that you decline invitations on my behalf.\"\n\n\"I wouldn't.\"\n\nHe took a breath.\n\n\"I prefer to know where I stand.\"\n\n\"You stand wherever you choose, Severus. You don't need to attend every gathering. And I don't need to pretend everyone I love has identical boundaries.\"\n\nSomething in his shoulders eased.\n\n\"I could perhaps tolerate a smaller visit,\" he said. \"At another time.\"\n\n\"Iroh would probably be delighted.\"\n\n\"Do not tell him I said delighted.\"\n\nI smiled.\n\nHe moved toward me, paused, and lifted a hand to my cheek. His touch was careful, but there was nothing uncertain about the way he looked at me.\n\n\"May I?\" he asked.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nThe kiss was brief, warm, and a little sharper when I smiled against his mouth. He rested his forehead against mine for one quiet beat.\n\nThe kettle began to whistle.\n\n\"That thing has the most appalling timing,\" I said.\n\n\"It has impeccable timing. You are simply distractible.\"\n\nThe tea was not as good as Iroh's.\n\nI told Severus so.\n\nHe informed me that my palate remained beyond repair.\n\nWe drank it anyway, together at a table where neither of us had been required to prove love by being anywhere we didn't wish to be.","word_count":2040},"after-always-48":{"number":48,"title":"The Lantern Vigil","subtitle":"The Lanternweald — fifth expansion pass","content":"Severus stood beneath the far arch with his coat buttoned to the throat and his hands in his pockets.\n\nI spotted him because everyone else was moving.\n\nThe Frostturn Lantern Walk had drawn people from every district, though not everyone had come for the same reason. Some carried lights painted with names. Some wore their best coats. Others had arrived straight from work, their boots still muddy from the ridge path. A child had brought a lantern shaped like an extremely unconvincing duck.\n\nGreg was playing a slow instrumental piece near the fountain.\n\nFor once, he hadn't been asked to fill every silence. The notes sat lightly in the air, leaving spaces for the sound of water beneath the old stones. People could listen, talk quietly, or ignore the music altogether.\n\nSeverus was doing none of those things. He was watching the lanterns with an expression I couldn't read.\n\nI started toward him.\n\nThen I stopped.\n\nWe had spent months learning that affection did not make another person's solitude a problem for me to solve. It would be easy to walk over and decide his silence was an invitation simply because I wanted to be beside him.\n\nI approached slowly enough that he could see me.\n\n\"Would you like company?\" I asked.\n\nHe glanced at me.\n\n\"That depends.\"\n\n\"On?\"\n\n\"Whether you intend to describe the emotional significance of every lantern.\"\n\n\"I was going to start with the duck.\"\n\nHe looked across the square.\n\n\"That creature is anatomically offensive.\"\n\nI smiled.\n\n\"Can I stand here?\"\n\nHe moved slightly to make room.\n\nIt was a small answer. It was enough.\n\nWe watched the procession begin. Neri checked the safe route, Edda directed people away from the steps still closed after the storm, and Pip made sure the temporary lantern stands did not narrow the walkway. The lower passage toward Tideglass remained under inspection. Nobody treated the festival as permission to ignore a closure.\n\nGreg's song changed key.\n\nSeverus's fingers twitched inside his pocket.\n\n\"Do you know the piece?\" I asked.\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"Do you like it?\"\n\nHe considered.\n\n\"It doesn't insist on being admired.\"\n\nI laughed softly.\n\n\"Greg would probably consider that high praise.\"\n\n\"Then please don't tell him.\"\n\nThe air smelled of cold stone, candle smoke, and the citrus tea Iroh was serving from a stand near the bakery. A lantern moved past us carrying a painted blue wave. Another held no mark at all.\n\nI thought of the third wartime letter Severus had chosen not to open.\n\n\"Are you remembering someone?\" I asked, then immediately wished I'd phrased it differently.\n\nHe looked at me.\n\n\"Several people.\"\n\n\"You don't have to tell me who.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\nWe stood quietly.\n\nAfter a while he said, \"For much of my life, remembrance was a public examination. People expected grief to resemble guilt, or loyalty, or confession. They wanted a correct answer.\"\n\n\"And here?\"\n\n\"Here there is a duck.\"\n\nI snorted.\n\nHe glanced at me.\n\n\"An important improvement.\"\n\nI covered my mouth.\n\nHe finally smiled.\n\nA small disagreement interrupted the line before it reached the corner.\n\nTwo volunteers had placed a lantern stand across the paved turning space outside the tea stall. Nobody had done it carelessly; the wind was stronger there, and the stand looked secure against the wall. But Edda stopped when she reached it.\n\n\"I can't turn around that,\" she said.\n\nOne of the volunteers looked toward the steps.\n\n\"We can carry it past you.\"\n\n\"I don't need to be carried past it,\" Edda answered. \"The stand needs to move.\"\n\nSeverus watched the conversation with his hands still in his pockets.\n\nThe volunteer flushed. \"We thought the space was wide enough.\"\n\n\"Then measure it,\" Edda said.\n\nPip fetched the chalk and a ruler, and the group moved the stand to a spot where it could be safely tethered without narrowing the route. It took less than ten minutes. The rest of the procession simply waited, talking quietly while Greg played the last notes of his piece.\n\nSeverus tilted his head.\n\n\"They corrected the design without declaring anyone incompetent.\"\n\n\"You're sounding impressed.\"\n\n\"I am assessing an interesting deviation from school staff meetings.\"\n\nI laughed.\n\nHe looked at Edda, who had resumed her place in the line on her own terms.\n\n\"When I taught,\" he said after a moment, \"I sometimes mistook embarrassment for a useful lesson.\"\n\nI let the admission stand. I could have asked for a list of his regrets. Instead I asked whether he wanted another cup of tea.\n\n\"Later,\" he said. \"I should like to see whether this ridiculous bird-shaped lantern survives the wind.\"\n\nIt did. A child held it with both hands and grinned as though the survival was entirely their own achievement.\n\nSeverus allowed himself a small smile.\n\nI loved that he had seen the practical work and what it meant, without needing the entire evening to become a public confession.\n\nThe child with the duck lantern stopped at the edge of the path. One side of the handle had bent, and the lantern hung at a sorry angle.\n\nSeverus went very still.\n\n\"Don't touch the hot frame,\" he said.\n\nThe child froze, eyes wide. I felt my own shoulders tighten at his tone. For one awful second I saw the old classroom, the sharp correction that could turn a mistake into shame.\n\nThen he noticed the child's face.\n\n\"The handle,\" he said, more quietly. \"Not you. The metal has come loose.\"\n\nEdda came over to check whether anyone had been burned. Nobody had. She suggested taking the lantern to the repair table before anyone tried to carry it again.\n\nSeverus crouched, though his knees clearly objected.\n\n\"Would you permit me to examine it once it cools?\"\n\nThe child nodded, then looked at me. I didn't speak for them. I moved closer to the repair table so they could see where the lantern would go.\n\nGreg's music stopped across the square. Not dramatically; he had simply finished his agreed set. The absence made it easier to hear Severus explain to the child which little tab had bent and why nobody should try to straighten it while it was hot.\n\n\"You sound like a teacher,\" I said when the child had gone to find their family.\n\n\"A deplorable habit.\"\n\n\"Not always.\"\n\nHe looked at me sharply, expecting mockery. I didn't give him any. I had watched him change the sentence before it became a wound. I couldn't know what it cost him, but I had seen him do it.\n\n\"Are you pleased with yourself?\" I asked.\n\n\"Not particularly.\"\n\n\"Then perhaps I am pleased with you.\"\n\nHe looked away. Under the hem of his cloak his fingers found mine, briefly and without show.\n\n\"An intolerable distinction,\" he muttered.\n\nWe stayed until the lantern cooled. When the child returned, the repair worker—not Severus—decided whether it was safe to lift again. Severus accepted that without a single argument. The duck wobbled happily away, its lopsided light shining on the stones.\n\nThe procession moved toward Jasmine Steps. Greg stopped playing and helped carry a lantern stand to the next corner. He had volunteered for one shift, not an entire night of unpaid music. I watched him exchange a few words with Iroh, then return to the square without a guitar in his hands.\n\nGreg was coiling a cable beside the fountain when Severus and I reached him. He had the loose, satisfied look of somebody who'd finished a set without breaking a string or being asked for an encore he didn't have time to play.\n\n“Hey,” Greg said. “You guys catch the last tune?”\n\n“I heard it,” Severus said. “The third phrase was excessively repetitive.”\n\nGreg looked wounded for half a second. “Aw, man. I worked hard on that excessive repetition.”\n\n“It improved when you stopped repeating it.”\n\nI pressed my lips together. Greg grinned.\n\n“That's either a compliment or the harshest review I've ever gotten from a guy in a cloak.”\n\n“Both are possible,” Severus replied.\n\nGreg looked down at the cable, then at the lantern near our feet. Its flame had blown sideways against a paper flap that had not been clipped down. He moved the lead out of the way before anyone could catch it with a boot.\n\n“Hey, that paper's getting kinda close to the hot part,” he said.\n\nSeverus didn't move the lantern. He asked the nearby volunteer who was responsible for the stand, waited for them to come over, and pointed out the flap. The volunteer clipped it back and moved the light to a safer angle. Greg watched the correction with the same attention he gave a badly placed microphone.\n\n“See?” he said. “That's more my kind of problem. I know when something's going to squeal.”\n\n“An admirable talent,” Severus said.\n\n“Did you just—”\n\n“No.”\n\nGreg laughed and went back to his case. I could feel Severus looking at me.\n\n“You are enjoying this,” he said.\n\n“Immensely.”\n\n“Try not to make a habit of it.”\n\nI took his hand under the edge of his cloak. He squeezed once, almost in spite of himself. Across the square Greg was already telling someone a terrible joke about a lantern that wanted to be a spotlight. Severus made a noise of profound disapproval, and I loved that he had not borrowed a single word of Greg's humor to express it.\n\nSeverus followed my gaze.\n\n\"Your other companions appear to be managing.\"\n\n\"They are.\"\n\nHe nodded.\n\nI waited for the old sharp remark. It didn't come.\n\nWe had spoken about my ethical non-monogamy and preference for polyamory often enough that he no longer treated every shared public event as a test of whether I would choose him above someone else. But understanding didn't erase the need for small negotiations.\n\n\"Do you want to walk with me?\" I asked. \"Or would you rather stay here?\"\n\nHe looked down the lit path.\n\n\"Walk. Slowly.\"\n\nWe joined the back of the procession. Nobody asked him to carry a lantern. Nobody tried to make him speak about the war. We passed the workshop lights at Brasswatch, then turned toward the safe ridge above Tideglass.\n\nMaui was helping a family settle blankets onto a bench, making a ridiculous show of lifting the entire stack one-handed. He spotted us, swept into a mock bow from a respectful distance, then let the family decide where the last blanket belonged. He didn't call us over.\n\nSeverus noticed.\n\n\"He's learning restraint.\"\n\n\"Painfully.\"\n\n\"A remarkable achievement.\"\n\nI smiled.\n\nAt the cove, I hung a small lantern with an open amber circle beside the others. It wasn't mine to explain. Severus watched me place it on the hook and step back.\n\n\"You left it blank,\" he said.\n\n\"Not blank. Unnamed.\"\n\nHe considered that distinction.\n\n\"Very well.\"\n\nHe took a folded scrap of paper from his pocket. I pretended not to notice until he held it out.\n\nIt was a single line, written in his precise handwriting.\n\nFOR THOSE I FAILED TO ASK.\n\nI looked at him.\n\n\"You want this on a lantern?\"\n\n\"No.\" His voice was quiet. \"I wanted you to know I had something to say. I don't want it displayed.\"\n\nI folded the paper carefully and gave it back.\n\n\"Then it's yours.\"\n\nHe closed his fingers around it.\n\nThe wind off the sea was colder than it had been in the square. I shivered. Without comment, Severus opened the side of his cloak and drew me close enough to share its shelter.\n\n\"Is this acceptable?\" he asked.\n\n\"Very.\"\n\nHe looked down at me.\n\n\"You appear to be smiling.\"\n\n\"I am.\"\n\n\"An unwise reaction to such an undignified arrangement.\"\n\n\"Extremely.\"\n\nHis hand rested against my back. He leaned close, paused, and let me close the last distance myself. The kiss was quiet enough to belong to us, not the crowd.\n\nWhen we turned toward the path, Greg began another instrumental somewhere beyond the ridge.\n\nSeverus listened for a moment.\n\n\"Tell him,\" he said reluctantly, \"that the second piece was better.\"\n\n\"Would you like to tell him yourself?\"\n\nHe looked offended.\n\n\"Don't become ambitious.\"\n\nI laughed.\n\nThe night ended without a speech from Severus, a public confession, or a declaration that he'd been transformed by festival lights.\n\nHe walked home beside me, our hands brushing inside the shelter of his cloak.\n\nI didn't need the silence to mean anything more than he had chosen to share it.","word_count":2056},"after-always-05":{"number":5,"title":"Instructions in the Margin","subtitle":"After Always · Chapter 5 of 40","content":"The book was wedged behind a row of cracked cauldrons and covered in enough dust to suggest that even Severus Snape had forgotten it existed.\n\nI pulled it free and sneezed.\n\nFrom the other side of the storeroom, Snape said, “If you contaminate the ingredients, I will have you removed.”\n\n“With what authority?”\n\n“Personal conviction.”\n\n“Terrifying.”\n\nI wiped the cover with my sleeve.\n\nAdvanced Potion-Making.\n\nThe title meant nothing unusual until I opened it.\n\nThen I saw the handwriting.\n\nSmall. Dense. Slanting. Corrections packed into margins. Ingredients crossed out and replaced. Brewing times adjusted by minutes. Tiny observations threaded between printed instructions like a second, more intelligent book growing inside the first.\n\nI stopped breathing for a moment.\n\nSnape heard the silence.\n\n“What?”\n\nI held up the book.\n\nHe went still.\n\n“Oh,” he said.\n\nIt was the least articulate thing I had heard from him.\n\nI looked back at the page.\n\n“Crush instead of cut.”\n\n“Give me that.”\n\n“Clockwise after every seventh counterclockwise turn.”\n\n“Now.”\n\n“You corrected the textbook.”\n\n“It was wrong.”\n\n“You were sixteen.”\n\n“It remained wrong regardless of my age.”\n\nHe crossed the room faster than he should have and took the book from my hands.\n\nHis face had shut down, but not before I saw embarrassment.\n\nActual embarrassment.\n\nIt was astonishing.\n\n“You're blushing,” I said.\n\n“I am not.”\n\n“You are.”\n\n“My circulation is affected by medication.”\n\n“Of course.”\n\n“Do you wish to continue working here?”\n\n“Desperately.”\n\nHe tucked the book under his arm.\n\nI should have let it go.\n\nInstead I said, “That is brilliant work.”\n\nSnape stopped.\n\nThe silence changed.\n\nI had praised him before, lightly, usually to irritate him. This was different. I meant it without humor.\n\nHe did not turn around.\n\n“The printed instructions wasted sopophorous juice,” he said.\n\n“That doesn't make the correction less brilliant.”\n\n“It is an adjustment.”\n\n“It's an adjustment a published author failed to find.”\n\n“Published authors fail to find many things.”\n\n“That sounded personal.”\n\n“Most truths do.”\n\nHe placed the book on a high shelf behind his desk.\n\nI returned to the cauldrons.\n\nTen minutes later, he brought it back.\n\nHe set it beside my hand.\n\n“You may examine it,” he said.\n\nI looked up.\n\n“Under supervision.”\n\n“Naturally.”\n\n“And you will not remove it from this room.”\n\n“I wouldn't.”\n\n“And you will not speak of the contents to Potter.”\n\nI blinked.\n\n“Harry Potter?”\n\nSnape's expression sharpened.\n\n“How many Potters do you imagine I mean?”\n\n“I don't know. You're very committed to grudges. There could be a list.”\n\nHis mouth twitched.\n\n“Why would I speak to him about it?”\n\n“Because he used it.”\n\nI stared at the book.\n\nThen at Snape.\n\n“The Boy Who Lived learned Potions from your school notes?”\n\n“Do not phrase it like that.”\n\n“How should I phrase it?”\n\n“He stole my textbook.”\n\n“Was your name in it?”\n\n“No.”\n\n“Then technically—”\n\n“Do not.”\n\nI laughed.\n\nSeverus laid the book on a clean square of cloth rather than directly on the workbench. He checked the hinges of the binding with two fingers, then positioned a weight so the pages would stay open without strain.\n\n\"You treat it like a rare artifact,\" I said.\n\n\"It has survived careless owners, a war, and an alarming number of students. I refuse to let you finish it off with greasy fingertips.\"\n\n\"I washed my hands.\"\n\n\"You touched a cauldron.\"\n\n\"An innocent cauldron.\"\n\n\"There is no such thing.\"\n\nI found a loose strip of parchment tucked into the back. The handwriting was smaller than the notes in the margins, with one calculation crossed out so heavily that the page had nearly torn.\n\n\"Was this an experiment?\"\n\n\"A failed one,\" he said.\n\n\"You kept it?\"\n\n\"Failures are instructive, provided one does not publish them as triumphs.\"\n\nHe reached for the strip, then stopped. \"You may read the measurements. Not the incantation on the reverse.\"\n\nI handed it back with the unmarked side facing him. The distinction mattered: he had given me access to part of his past, not declared the whole thing common property.\n\nWhen he returned it to the book, his knuckles brushed mine. The contact was brief and entirely unnecessary. His eyes flicked toward my face, and I pretended not to notice the color warming the tops of his ears.\n\nThen he invited me to examine the next page without adding a single new restriction. The permission felt more intimate than the accidental touch.\n\nThe book turned out to be more revealing than any confession Snape might have offered.\n\nNot because it contained secrets about his life. It did not. It contained impatience.\n\nYoung Snape had hated inefficiency.\n\nHe had questioned every instruction. Tested every assumption. Refused to respect authority that could not survive evidence. He had written insults in the margins beside clumsy methods, sometimes only a single question mark sharp enough to function as contempt.\n\nThere were spells too.\n\nSome harmless.\n\nSome clever.\n\nSome not.\n\nI paused over one marked for enemies.\n\nSnape saw where my eyes had stopped.\n\nHis face went very quiet.\n\n“Later,” he said.\n\nNot never.\n\nLater.\n\nI closed the book.\n\n“Agreed.”\n\nHe seemed surprised.\n\nI returned to the safer pages.\n\nA note beside a burn-healing paste recommended adding powdered silver last instead of first. I frowned.\n\n“That would reduce scarring.”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Why isn't this standard practice?”\n\n“Because healers repeat what they are taught.”\n\n“So do potioneers.”\n\n“Bad ones.”\n\nI looked at the pale marks on his throat.\n\n“Did you use it?”\n\nHis hand rose unconsciously to his collar.\n\n“Not initially.”\n\n“Because you were unconscious.”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Pomfrey?”\n\n“Eventually.”\n\n“Did it help?”\n\n“A little.”\n\nThe answer was softer than I expected.\n\nI turned another page.\n\nThe margins became more crowded in later chapters, as though the boy writing them had grown bolder. There were entire replacement methods squeezed between lines.\n\n“What did your professor think?” I asked.\n\n“That I was insufferable.”\n\n“Accurate.”\n\n“Indeed.”\n\n“And talented?”\n\nHe did not answer.\n\nI looked up.\n\nSnape was watching the book, not me.\n\n“No one said it?”\n\n“That is an absurd question.”\n\n“Is it?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\nI waited.\n\nHe turned away.\n\nThat was answer enough.\n\nSomething in me ached unexpectedly.\n\nNot pity. I had promised myself not to reduce him to a collection of wounds. But there was something bleak about imagining a boy filling margins with discoveries and learning that brilliance could be ignored if the person carrying it was difficult enough to dislike.\n\nThen again, Snape had likely made himself very easy to dislike.\n\nBoth things could be true.\n\nI tapped the page.\n\n“This should be published.”\n\nHe looked at me as if I had suggested public nudity.\n\n“No.”\n\n“Why?”\n\n“Because I said so.”\n\n“That is not a reason. That's a classroom shortcut.”\n\nHis eyes narrowed.\n\n“You think I don't recognize the difference?”\n\n“I think you use authority when explanation would cost more.”\n\nHe became very still.\n\nI had done it again: stepped too close to whatever he kept behind the doors.\n\nBut this time he did not reach for his wand.\n\nHe sat down.\n\n“The notes are mine,” he said. “Some were written by a child who believed cleverness was sufficient protection. Some by an adolescent who mistook cruelty for strength. Some are useful. Some are dangerous. I have no desire to package that mixture for strangers to admire.”\n\nI considered him.\n\n“That is a reason.”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“I still think the useful parts deserve daylight.”\n\n“They have survived thirty years without it.”\n\n“So have you.”\n\nHis eyes snapped to mine.\n\nThe sentence hung between us.\n\nI had not planned it.\n\nNeither of us seemed to know what to do with it.\n\nAt last Snape said, “A regrettable comparison.”\n\n“Maybe.”\n\nI turned back to the book.\n\nHe let me keep reading.\n\nNear the back, I found a potion I did not recognize. There was no printed recipe, only his handwriting across a blank page.\n\nThe ingredients formed a protective tonic: dittany, asphodel, bezoar extract, a carefully measured venom neutralizer, and a stabilizing agent usually used in long-duration brews.\n\nMy pulse quickened.\n\n“This is the antivenom.”\n\nHe said nothing.\n\n“The one you were taking before the battle.”\n\nStill nothing.\n\nI read the notes.\n\nDOSAGE: 7 DAYS MINIMUM FOR TISSUE SATURATION.\n\nLIMITED EFFICACY AGAINST UNKNOWN SERPENT VENOMS.\n\nDO NOT RELY ON AS SOLE TREATMENT.\n\nBLOOD-LOSS RISK UNAFFECTED.\n\nI looked at him.\n\n“You knew.”\n\n“I suspected.”\n\n“That Nagini might be used against you.”\n\n“I had observed her diet.”\n\nThe dry delivery made the horror worse.\n\n“You prepared for your own murder.”\n\n“I prepared for a range of foreseeable outcomes.”\n\n“That's a very Snape way to say it.”\n\n“An astonishing observation, given that I am Snape.”\n\nI traced the edge of the page without touching the ink.\n\n“You wrote not to rely on it as sole treatment.”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“But you went anyway.”\n\nHis eyes hardened.\n\n“There was nowhere else to go.”\n\n“That isn't true.”\n\n“It was at the time.”\n\nI wanted to argue.\n\nInstead I looked at the formula.\n\nA potion designed by a man who expected death and still made one last technical correction against it.\n\n“You saved yourself,” I said.\n\nSnape's expression turned strange.\n\n“No.”\n\n“The potion kept you alive long enough to be found.”\n\n“That is not the same thing.”\n\n“It is from where I'm standing.”\n\nHe closed the book.\n\n“Then stand somewhere else.”\n\nThe words were sharp, but his voice broke slightly on the last one.\n\nPain.\n\nThroat, memory, or both.\n\nI did not push.\n\nWe spent the rest of the afternoon cataloguing cauldrons.\n\nJust before dinner, he placed the textbook in my hands.\n\n“Take it,” he said.\n\nI stared at him.\n\n“To your quarters. You wished to examine the useful sections.”\n\n“What about your rule?”\n\n“I am revising it.”\n\n“Why?”\n\nHis expression became familiar sarcasm again, a coat he could put on quickly.\n\n“Because watching you read is intolerably distracting.”\n\nI held the book against my chest.\n\n“I'll bring it back tomorrow.”\n\n“You will bring it back tonight if there is so much as a bent corner.”\n\n“Yes, Professor.”\n\nHis eyes flashed.\n\nI smiled.\n\nHe looked away first.\n\nThat evening, I read the margins until after midnight.\n\nNot because I wanted to solve Severus Snape.\n\nI was beginning to understand that he was not a puzzle placed in front of me for entertainment.\n\nI read because the mind in those pages was fierce, impatient, inventive, wounded, arrogant, and alive.\n\nAnd because, for reasons I was not yet willing to name, he had trusted me with it.","word_count":1736},"after-always-27":{"number":27,"title":"The Kiss in the Storeroom","subtitle":"After Always · Chapter 27 of 40","content":"The first time Severus Snape kissed me, we were arguing about powdered unicorn horn.\n\nI wish that were more dignified.\n\nIt was not.\n\nThe hearing was the next morning. Neither of us had slept. Rather than admit this, we had gone to the dungeon before sunrise to complete a stock transfer that could easily have waited a week.\n\nI was checking a shipment from Diagon Alley.\n\nSnape was correcting me.\n\n“This grade is unacceptable.”\n\n“The supplier lists it as medicinal.”\n\n“The supplier is either lying or blind.”\n\nI held the packet under the lamp.\n\n“It looks fine.”\n\n“It is too coarse.”\n\n“It's powder.”\n\n“It is gravel pretending to be powder.”\n\n“You're nervous.”\n\nHis eyes narrowed.\n\n“Do not change the subject.”\n\n“You're bullying unicorn horn because you're nervous.”\n\n“I am identifying substandard material.”\n\n“Personally.”\n\n“Unlike you, I take standards seriously.”\n\nThere it was.\n\nThe cheap cut.\n\nNot as cruel as the letter day.\n\nStill deliberate.\n\nI set down the packet.\n\n“Try again.”\n\nHis expression changed.\n\n“What?”\n\n“You're afraid. Fine. You're angry. Fine. You want to complain about the supplier until sunrise, also fine. You don't get to use me as a practice target.”\n\nHis jaw tightened.\n\n“I did not—”\n\n“Yes, you did.”\n\nSilence.\n\nThe old pattern stood between us like an open door.\n\nHe could deny.\n\nEscalate.\n\nMake me prove the injury before he acknowledged it.\n\nInstead Snape closed his eyes.\n\n“You are right.”\n\nThe anger drained out of me so quickly it left me tired.\n\n“I hate when you do that.”\n\nHis eyes opened.\n\n“Apologize?”\n\n“Improve.”\n\nHis mouth twitched.\n\n“I am sorry.”\n\n“Thank you.”\n\nWe stood on opposite sides of the storeroom.\n\nIt was narrow, lined floor to ceiling with jars and packets. One lamp hung overhead. The air smelled of dried herbs, stone, and the faint metallic sweetness of unicorn horn.\n\nSnape rubbed his hand over his face.\n\n“I should not be here.”\n\n“At Hogwarts?”\n\n“In this room.”\n\n“Because of the horn?”\n\n“Because I am tired enough to be careless.”\n\nI softened.\n\n“Then let's stop.”\n\nHe looked at me.\n\n“Just like that?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“The inventory—”\n\n“Will survive.”\n\n“The hearing—”\n\n“Will happen whether these shelves are finished or not.”\n\nHe leaned back against the wall.\n\nFor once, Snape looked exactly as tired as he was.\n\nI stepped closer.\n\nNot too close.\n\n“Do you want company?” I asked.\n\nHis gaze lifted.\n\n“Yes.”\n\nSo I leaned against the opposite shelf.\n\nWe stood there, two exhausted adults hiding from a government hearing between jars of beetle eyes.\n\nIt was ridiculous.\n\nI began to laugh.\n\nSnape looked offended.\n\n“What?”\n\n“Nothing.”\n\n“Do not.”\n\n“Tomorrow, half the Wizengamot is going to debate your soul, and right now you're sulking next to dried nettles.”\n\nHis mouth twitched.\n\n“When phrased by an idiot, most situations sound absurd.”\n\n“There you are.”\n\nHe laughed.\n\nSoftly.\n\nThe tension broke.\n\nThen didn't.\n\nBecause when the laughter faded, we were still looking at each other.\n\nStill too awake.\n\nToo aware.\n\nWeeks of almost-touch lived in the small room with us.\n\nHis fingers on my cheek.\n\nMy mouth against his knuckles.\n\nThree steps under an umbrella.\n\nA hand held across a table marked by old allegiance.\n\nSnape's eyes dropped to my mouth.\n\nThis time he did not immediately look away.\n\nMy pulse changed.\n\nHe heard the shift in my breathing.\n\n“You are doing that again,” he said.\n\n“What?”\n\n“Looking at me as though I am about to become someone else.”\n\n“I don't want someone else.”\n\nThe words came easily.\n\nHis face changed.\n\n“I know what you are,” I continued. “As much as anyone can. I know what you've done. I know what you regret. I know you're capable of being cruel when frightened. I know you hate sweet tea and sleep badly and alphabetize at home.”\n\nHis eyes narrowed.\n\n“You swore never to weaponize that cupboard.”\n\n“I made no such promise.”\n\n“Untrustworthy.”\n\n“Terribly.”\n\nI stepped closer.\n\nHe did not move away.\n\n“I don't want a nicer version of you,” I said.\n\n“That is a foolish statement. I would prefer a nicer version of me.”\n\nI laughed softly.\n\n“Fine. I want you to become kinder. I don't want you to become generic.”\n\nHis mouth curved.\n\n“An important distinction.”\n\n“It is to me.”\n\nThe space between us narrowed.\n\nSnape's hand rose.\n\nHe did not touch me.\n\n“May I?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\nHis fingers settled against my jaw.\n\nThe contact was familiar now.\n\nThe intention was not.\n\n“May I kiss you?” he asked.\n\nEvery nerve in my body answered before my mouth did.\n\n“Yes.”\n\nHe leaned in slowly.\n\nFor a man known for precision, the first contact was almost uncertain.\n\nHis mouth touched mine.\n\nSoft.\n\nBrief.\n\nHe pulled back half an inch.\n\nI opened my eyes.\n\nHis were watching me.\n\n“Again?” I whispered.\n\nHis breath caught.\n\n“Yes?”\n\nI smiled.\n\n“Yes.”\n\nThe second kiss was different.\n\nStill careful.\n\nNot tentative.\n\nHis hand moved from my jaw to the back of my neck. Mine found the front of his coat, gripping fabric because I needed somewhere to put the sudden amount of feeling.\n\nHe kissed like he did most things once he committed: with concentrated attention.\n\nNo performance.\n\nNo hurry.\n\nEvery change asked through pause and pressure.\n\nI answered by moving closer.\n\nHis back met the shelf.\n\nA jar rattled.\n\nWe froze.\n\nI laughed against his mouth.\n\nSnape closed his eyes.\n\n“If we die beneath pickled newts,” he murmured, “I will haunt you.”\n\n“You'd haunt me anyway.”\n\n“Probably.”\n\nI kissed him again.\n\nHe made a low sound that traveled directly through me.\n\nMy hand rose toward his throat, then stopped.\n\nThe scar.\n\nHe felt the hesitation.\n\nHis eyes opened.\n\n“Ask,” he said.\n\n“Can I touch your neck?”\n\nFear flickered.\n\nThen trust.\n\n“Not the scar.”\n\n“Okay.”\n\nI let my fingertips rest against the uninjured side below his ear.\n\nHis pulse jumped beneath them.\n\nHe exhaled.\n\n“Still all right?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\nThe word was rough.\n\nI kissed him more gently.\n\nWe stayed close for another moment, listening for footsteps outside the storeroom. None came. Severus's fingertips rested at the edge of my sleeve, not my throat.\n\n\"The hearing,\" he said.\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"It begins in less than two hours.\"\n\n\"Also know that.\"\n\nHis mouth tightened. \"I have not suddenly become a better witness because you kissed me.\"\n\n\"I didn't kiss you to change your testimony.\"\n\nHe looked at me carefully, as though the answer required more examination than any ingredient in the room.\n\n\"Good,\" he said at last.\n\n\"And I didn't do it to make you a public declaration.\"\n\n\"An immense relief. I have no interest in being discussed between the powdered root and the pickled newts.\"\n\n\"You're being discussed with the newts already.\"\n\nHe closed his eyes. The sound he made might have been a groan, but his hand settled over mine. I felt a small, fierce pulse of desire followed by the simpler wish that the hearing would be fair. Neither feeling canceled the other.\n\n\"One thing,\" I said.\n\nHe waited.\n\n\"If you need space after tomorrow, ask. I won't decide that a kiss means I get to claim all of it.\"\n\n\"And if I wish to see you?\"\n\n\"You can ask that too.\"\n\nHe nodded once, formal and shaken and very much himself. I wanted to pull him into another kiss. I let him decide whether the moment was finished. When he finally stepped back, I was smiling so hard I could barely manage to look serious.\n\nThe jar had stopped rattling, but I was still laughing. Severus straightened his collar with an expression of deep personal outrage.\n\n\"That,\" he said, \"was an extremely poor location for a first kiss.\"\n\n\"You chose it.\"\n\n\"I was distracted by the substandard unicorn horn.\"\n\n\"Of course you were.\"\n\nHe tried to glare at me. It didn't quite work. I couldn't remember the last time I'd seen him look so young and so frightened at once.\n\nThe hearing was still waiting for us. We would need to walk into a room of people who had reasons to distrust him, some of them good reasons. A kiss wasn't going to alter his testimony or make anybody forget what he'd done.\n\n\"Do you regret it?\" I asked.\n\nHis face changed. \"No.\"\n\n\"Good.\"\n\n\"Do you?\"\n\n\"No. Though I would prefer our next romantic moment not be scheduled beside preserved amphibians.\"\n\nHe looked toward the shelf. \"An unreasonable standard for a potions master.\"\n\nI took a breath. \"Severus, I meant what I told you before. I'm ethically non-monogamous, and I prefer polyamory. I won't promise you're the only person I can ever love. I can promise I won't use intimacy to pressure you into being someone else.\"\n\n\"I know,\" he said. \"You have been painfully clear.\"\n\n\"And?\"\n\n\"And I chose to kiss you with that knowledge.\"\n\nHis answer made me feel warm in places that had nothing to do with the damp dungeon air. I stepped toward him again, but this time we both stopped before closing the distance. A bell rang overhead.\n\n\"That will be the delivery clerk,\" he said.\n\n\"The universe has terrible timing.\"\n\n\"On that, we agree.\"\n\nWe signed the rejected shipment into the proper ledger. He corrected my spelling with insulting satisfaction. I leaned against his shoulder for a moment and discovered he didn't move away.\n\nAt sunrise we would go to the hearing. Tonight we could have one more cup of tea and a kiss that belonged only to us, not to the arguments waiting outside the storeroom.\n\nWhen we finally separated, neither of us looked particularly dignified.\n\nSnape's hair had fallen across his face.\n\nMy coat was crooked.\n\nThe unicorn horn remained substandard.\n\nHe looked at the packet on the table.\n\n“Still gravel,” he said.\n\nI started laughing again.\n\nHe pressed his forehead briefly to mine.\n\nThat gesture almost affected me more than the kiss.\n\n“What now?” I asked.\n\nHis expression sobered.\n\n“Now we sleep.”\n\n“Separately?”\n\nColor rose at his cheeks.\n\nI had not meant it as pressure, only a question.\n\n“Yes,” he said.\n\n“Okay.”\n\n“And tomorrow—”\n\n“We go to the hearing.”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Together?”\n\nHe looked at me.\n\nThen held out his hand.\n\n“Together.”\n\nI took it.\n\nWe left the storeroom at sunrise.\n\nThe powdered unicorn horn stayed on the rejection pile.\n\nSome standards, apparently, remained non-negotiable.","word_count":1694},"after-always-45":{"number":45,"title":"The Terms of Us","subtitle":"The Lanternweald — expansion pass three","content":"Severus did not shout when I told him I had forgotten dinner.\n\nHe looked at the clock, then at the covered plates, and said, \"I see.\"\n\nThat was worse.\n\nThe plates were on the kitchen table at Spinner's End, protected by a warming charm that had kept the food from going cold. Severus had even put out the good tea service. I had promised him a quiet evening after the committee meeting. Instead, I'd stayed at Riverstone Terrace while Katara's clinic volunteers were preparing supplies for the South.\n\nI had sent a note.\n\nThe note had been delivered after dinner was supposed to begin.\n\n\"I'm sorry,\" I said.\n\nHe folded his napkin carefully.\n\n\"Forgetting is inconvenient. Informing me after the appointed hour is discourteous.\"\n\n\"You're right.\"\n\n\"Of course I am.\"\n\nI wanted to argue that the work had been urgent. It had been. I wanted to explain that Katara's departure was emotional for everyone involved. It was. None of those facts had made the note arrive on time.\n\n\"I should have told you the moment I realized I couldn't make it,\" I said. \"I didn't.\"\n\nSeverus looked at me for several seconds.\n\n\"Thank you.\"\n\nHe did not say he forgave me. He did not have to.\n\nI took off my coat and hung it beside his. The hall was damp from rain, and the lamps burned with the familiar yellow light that made the chipped wallpaper look almost warm.\n\n\"Would you like me to leave?\" I asked.\n\nHe seemed genuinely surprised.\n\n\"No. I would like you to stop standing in the doorway as though I intend to expel you.\"\n\nThat was enough of an invitation.\n\nI sat across from him.\n\nWe ate dinner together, but we didn't pretend the issue had gone away. He asked about the supplies. I told him what I could without sharing the clinic's private medical information. He offered a correction to the preservation method for one herb, and I wrote it down.\n\nThen he set his fork aside.\n\n\"Are our arrangements still what you want?\"\n\nI looked up.\n\n\"Which arrangements?\"\n\n\"Your relationships. My place among them.\"\n\nI had known the question would come eventually. I was only surprised he had chosen to ask it so plainly.\n\n\"Yes,\" I said. \"I'm ethically non-monogamous, and I still prefer polyamory. I care about you, and I care about other people. None of that has changed.\"\n\nHe watched my face.\n\n\"I am not asking you to change it.\"\n\n\"Then what are you asking?\"\n\nHe looked down at his hands.\n\n\"Whether I have mistaken my willingness to be discreet for a willingness to be forgotten.\"\n\nIt hurt to hear, because it was precise.\n\n\"I don't want you to feel forgotten.\"\n\n\"That was not my question.\"\n\nI took a breath.\n\n\"No. You haven't mistaken anything. I assumed you'd understand because you're private. That's unfair.\"\n\nHe nodded once.\n\nSeverus was not a person who wanted grand declarations in crowded rooms. He did not want to attend every feast or make the names of my partners part of a public inventory. He did want to know when I was coming home. He wanted the freedom to decline gatherings without being interpreted as jealous or ashamed. He wanted me to ask rather than presume that a missed evening could be absorbed without consequence.\n\nI took out my notebook.\n\nHe raised an eyebrow.\n\n\"Must everything become documentation?\"\n\n\"Only the things I keep getting wrong.\"\n\n\"An extensive volume.\"\n\nI gave him a look.\n\nHe almost smiled.\n\nWe made a list, though it was more a conversation than a contract.\n\nHe wanted advance notice when plans changed, unless a genuine emergency made it impossible. I wanted him to tell me when he needed reassurance instead of delivering increasingly elegant insults until I guessed correctly. He wanted privacy around details of my other relationships, not because he disapproved but because he considered intimate lives a poor subject for casual conversation. I wanted him to understand that being discreet about us didn't mean hiding that I cared for him.\n\n\"Are we allowed to be seen together?\" I asked.\n\nHe looked insulted.\n\n\"Certainly.\"\n\n\"At a public tea tasting?\"\n\n\"Do not push your luck.\"\n\nI laughed.\n\nHe rolled his eyes and, because he knew me, offered his hand across the table.\n\nI took it.\n\n\"And what of my time?\" he asked.\n\n\"What about it?\"\n\n\"You have spent a considerable amount of energy arranging yourself around everyone else's needs. What do you require?\"\n\nI had been prepared for his hurt. I had not prepared for his concern.\n\n\"I need to be able to say no,\" I admitted. \"Even to people I love. I need writing time. Quiet time. And I need to know that if I make a mistake, we can talk about it without treating the entire relationship as a failure.\"\n\nSeverus squeezed my fingers.\n\n\"An eminently sensible set of demands.\"\n\n\"Really?\"\n\n\"Do not sound so surprised. You are occasionally sensible.\"\n\n\"Should I note the date?\"\n\n\"Please don't.\"\n\nI turned the calendar toward him. The pages were crowded with more names than his careful handwriting usually tolerated, but none of those names was a measure of how much I cared.\n\n\"Which parts of the week are yours to ask about?\" I said.\n\nHe considered. \"The parts involving a promise you have made to me. And the parts where an unexpected change affects my own plans.\"\n\n\"That sounds reasonable.\"\n\n\"It is deeply unsettling to be praised for basic competence.\"\n\nI smiled. \"What about your time? If you need a night alone, I want you to be able to say so without having to invent work.\"\n\nHe looked at the tea service.\n\n\"I may require practice.\"\n\n\"So might I.\"\n\nWe wrote a small note in the margin: *Confirm, don't assume.* It wasn't a contract that controlled where either of us went. It was a reminder that I could spend time with Katara, Greg, Iroh or anyone else I loved and still owe Severus honesty about a dinner we'd actually planned.\n\n\"I dislike calendars,\" he announced.\n\n\"You have three.\"\n\n\"I dislike this calendar.\"\n\n\"Because it includes people who aren't you?\"\n\n\"Because you've drawn an enormous star beside Saturday and written 'maybe.'\"\n\n\"That's your invitation to the tea tasting.\"\n\nHe stared at it.\n\n\"You had better not expect me to enjoy it.\"\n\n\"I wouldn't dream of it.\"\n\nHe reached across the table for my hand. I could feel how carefully he chose the gesture, how much he was asking without saying it. His fingertips were warm. I moved closer.\n\n\"May I kiss you?\" I asked.\n\nHe looked offended by the question for approximately half a second.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nIt was slow, almost shy, and made me forget why I'd been annoyed with the calendar. When we drew apart he rested his forehead against mine.\n\n\"Saturday,\" he said. \"If I am available.\"\n\nI laughed. \"I'll take that as a maybe.\"\n\nHe made a quiet sound that might have been a chuckle. The rain continued tapping at the window while we put the calendar away, leaving the rest of the evening open to whatever we both chose.\n\nThe rain grew heavier outside. It tapped the window in uneven rhythms, and the candle flame bent every time the old house shifted.\n\nSeverus stood to clear the plates. I helped without asking him to accept a performance of penitence. We washed the dishes and put the good cups away. Small ordinary tasks had a way of letting an argument settle into its proper size.\n\nIn the hallway, he stopped me.\n\n\"Jasper.\"\n\n\"Yes?\"\n\n\"I had been afraid that asking for more of your attention would mean asking you to love someone else less.\"\n\nI went still.\n\n\"That isn't how it works,\" I said. \"We can discuss time and boundaries without comparing people as if they're numbers on a scale.\"\n\n\"I know that in theory.\"\n\n\"And in practice?\"\n\nHe considered the question.\n\n\"In practice, I am learning.\"\n\nHe looked at the clock before he looked at me.\n\n\"I spent many years making myself unavailable,\" he said. \"It was an efficient way to avoid being disappointed.\"\n\n\"Sounds lonely.\"\n\n\"It was frequently preferable to the alternatives.\"\n\nHe said it without drama. I remembered the dust on his old books, the careful way he guarded letters that belonged to other people, and the work he could do for hours without anyone watching.\n\n\"I don't want to turn being available into another requirement,\" I told him. \"You're allowed to close the door even when I could come through it.\"\n\nHe considered that, then drew a narrow line beneath Saturday's tentative mark. He wrote PRIVATE above Sunday in his precise hand.\n\n\"Does that mean no visitors?\"\n\n\"It means I would prefer to decide before anyone arrives.\"\n\n\"That sounds very reasonable.\"\n\n\"Your approval is intolerable.\"\n\nI smiled. \"Then I'll be intolerable on purpose.\"\n\nWhen he put away the pen, he didn't hide the small satisfaction in his expression. I leaned forward, aware of his attention settling on my mouth. He remained perfectly still while I chose how close to come.\n\n\"Would you like me to stay tonight?\" I asked.\n\n\"Yes.\" The answer came quickly enough to embarrass him.\n\nI stayed. The door to the rest of the house remained unlocked, and the evening remained ours because we had both chosen it.\n\nI stepped closer.\n\n\"Me too.\"\n\nHe lifted a hand to my face. There was hesitation in the movement, not because he doubted I cared, but because he understood the value of asking even after a long history together.\n\n\"May I kiss you?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nHis lips met mine, slow at first, then warmer when I rested a hand against his chest. I could feel his heart beneath the fabric, steady and human and nothing like the frightening legend other people had made of him.\n\nWhen we parted, he kept his hand against my cheek.\n\n\"Tomorrow,\" he said, \"I would like breakfast.\"\n\n\"With me?\"\n\n\"An appalling deduction.\"\n\n\"I can be here.\"\n\n\"If that changes, tell me before the porridge is ruined.\"\n\nI laughed against his shoulder.\n\nWe spent the rest of the evening together, with the rain closing the world to one small kitchen and one open doorway. No one else had to disappear for that moment to matter.\n\nThe next morning I arrived when I said I would.\n\nSeverus had laid out two bowls.\n\nThere was no celebration. No dramatic proof that we'd solved ethical non-monogamy once and for all.\n\nWe ate breakfast, and when I left for Moonroot Archive, I told him when I expected to return.\n\nThat was the beginning of keeping the terms of us.","word_count":1760},"after-always-06":{"number":6,"title":"A Question of Antidotes","subtitle":"After Always · Chapter 6 of 40","content":"The sixth hidden phial tried to bite me.\n\nBefore the glass could strike again, Severus pulled a heavy ledger from beneath the bench. Its corners were scarred by years of impatient handling. He compared the ink on the vial's outer label with a notation in the ledger and made a displeased sound.\n\n“What?”\n\n“The mixture has been catalogued under a name it has never had. Whoever wrote this assumes that two red reagents with similar behavior are interchangeable.”\n\n“They aren't?”\n\n“If they were, we would not need to bother studying them.”\n\nHe turned the page so I could see the old formula. Part of the entry was crossed through, not with a dramatic warning but with the blunt word *unverified*. Beneath it he'd listed three things he hadn't been able to reproduce.\n\n“You kept the mistakes,” I said.\n\n“I kept the observations. The failures are more useful than the account of a perfect result that never occurred.”\n\nI held the shield while he inspected the seal. The potion's little red mouth opened against the glass, soundless and ugly. When I moved the silver spoon too close, he stopped me with a sharp, immediate instruction.\n\n“Back.”\n\nI obeyed. After the reaction subsided, he lowered his wand.\n\n“That was a safety instruction,” he said. “Not an invitation to prove you trust me by taking a risk.”\n\n“Good. Because my trust doesn't come with a willingness to be bitten by laboratory equipment.”\n\nHis mouth twitched. He wrote down the time, the temperature and the angle of the spoon. The unknown remained unknown; at least it had stopped pretending to be familiar.\n\nNot metaphorically.\n\nThe moment I broke the outer seal, the potion rose from the glass in a narrow red strand, formed something remarkably like a mouth, and snapped at the silver testing spoon.\n\nI jerked my hand back.\n\nSnape did not.\n\nHis wand moved once.\n\nThe red strand froze in midair, shivered, and dropped back into the phial.\n\n“Impressive,” I said.\n\n“Careless.”\n\n“I meant the potion.”\n\n“So did I.”\n\nI looked at him.\n\nHe had already turned away, reaching for a containment bell.\n\n“Did you know it would do that?”\n\n“No.”\n\n“You don't sound surprised.”\n\n“I have found surprise to be of limited practical value.”\n\n“That explains your parties.”\n\n“I do not attend parties.”\n\n“That explains them even more.”\n\nThe corner of his mouth threatened movement.\n\nHe placed the glass bell over the phial and sealed it with three flicks of his wand. The potion struck the inside of the dome once, then settled.\n\n“Blood-reactive,” he said.\n\n“I didn't cut myself.”\n\n“No, but you handled the bitterroot earlier.”\n\nI looked at my fingertips. “Trace residue?”\n\n“Possibly.”\n\n“That is extraordinarily sensitive.”\n\n“It was designed to be.”\n\n“For what?”\n\nHis expression changed.\n\nThere were moments when Snape became so still that I could almost see the decision happening behind his eyes: how much truth, how much omission, how much protection.\n\n“Interrogation,” he said.\n\nThe word landed heavily.\n\n“The draught was intended to react to elevated pulse and blood chemistry. Fear, pain, exertion. It would alter color when a subject became sufficiently distressed.”\n\n“A lie detector?”\n\n“A crude one.”\n\n“Voldemort wanted it?”\n\n“One of his lieutenants did. The Dark Lord approved.”\n\nI looked at the phial.\n\n“What did you do to it?”\n\nSnape's gaze sharpened.\n\n“You assume I did something.”\n\n“The other five were sabotaged.”\n\n“Patterns can encourage laziness.”\n\n“So can evasiveness.”\n\nHe ignored that.\n\nI ran the residual magic reader along the bell. It chimed twice, then shrieked.\n\n“Interesting.”\n\n“Move.”\n\nSnape reached past me.\n\nI caught his wrist before his fingers touched the bell.\n\nWe both froze.\n\nRule: no touching without warning unless someone was falling.\n\nApparently I had discovered an amendment for someone about to touch a blood-reactive potion.\n\nHis skin was cool. His pulse was not.\n\n“Don't,” I said.\n\nHis eyes dropped to my hand.\n\nI released him at once.\n\n“What did the reader show?” he asked.\n\n“Two layers. One old, one recent.”\n\n“Recent?”\n\n“Within months.”\n\n“That is impossible.”\n\n“Why?”\n\n“Because I brewed it years ago.”\n\nWe looked at each other.\n\nThen at the phial.\n\nSomeone had tampered with it after Snape.\n\nThe room changed around us.\n\nUntil then, the audit had been archaeology. Dangerous archaeology, but still the sorting of a finished war.\n\nNow the past had a fresh fingerprint.\n\nSnape drew his wand.\n\nThis time I did too.\n\n“Who had access?” I asked.\n\n“During the last school year? The Carrows. Selected Death Eaters. Anyone Voldemort chose to send.”\n\n“After the battle?”\n\n“Ministry evidence teams.”\n\n“Reconstruction staff?”\n\n“Not to the sealed stores.”\n\nI thought of the wax. The official stamps. The careless question mark on the cabinet.\n\n“Unless someone broke a seal and replaced it.”\n\nSnape's face became unpleasantly focused.\n\n“Your instrument.”\n\nI handed it to him.\n\nHe scanned the phial, then the crate, then the shelf where we had found it. The needle quivered near the back wall.\n\n“Again,” he said.\n\nI joined him.\n\nWe moved every jar.\n\nBehind the shelf was a narrow crack in the stone.\n\nSnape pressed his wand tip against it and whispered a spell I did not recognize.\n\nThe mortar glowed green.\n\n“Concealment charm,” he said.\n\nSeverus did not touch the crack again. He drew a square of parchment from his pocket and sketched the shelf, the phial and the little gap in the wall in three precise lines.\n\n\"Should we call someone?\" I asked.\n\n\"We should record what we have found before anyone else can improve the story.\"\n\n\"Improve?\"\n\n\"Misremember. Embellish. Decide the evidence proves their favored villain did it.\"\n\nHe held the parchment out for my signature as a witness. I wrote the time and exactly what I had seen, refusing to describe a hand I had not seen or a person I had not identified.\n\n\"Very good,\" he said.\n\nI stared at him. \"Was that praise?\"\n\n\"A momentary lapse. Do not grow accustomed.\"\n\nHe placed the phial in an unmarked protective box and sealed it with wax. I asked whether the Ministry would know the container had been opened.\n\n\"It will, if the inventory is handled correctly.\"\n\n\"And if it isn't?\"\n\n\"Then we create a second record.\"\n\nHe looked exhausted in the harsh lamplight. I could see the strain of old pain in his throat when he swallowed. I wanted to reach for him, but this was not a moment that belonged to my wish to comfort him.\n\n\"Can I do anything?\" I asked.\n\n\"Yes. Stop trying to anticipate my conclusions.\"\n\n\"An impossible request.\"\n\n\"Then try the attainable one. Bring me clean paper.\"\n\nI did. His mouth twitched as he accepted it. The room was full of things we could not yet explain, and that did not entitle either of us to invent certainty.\n\n“Yours?”\n\n“No.”\n\nThe wall opened.\n\nInside was a cavity no larger than a bread box.\n\nIt held four more phials.\n\nNone were his.\n\nI knew because he swore.\n\nIt was the first time I had heard him do it.\n\nNot loudly. Not creatively. But with enough feeling that I almost laughed before remembering what we were looking at.\n\nEach phial bore a thin silver mark etched into the glass: a hooked A.\n\n“Alecto?” I asked.\n\nHis jaw tightened.\n\n“Possibly.”\n\n“What's in them?”\n\n“I do not know.”\n\nThe admission came reluctantly.\n\nI liked it more than I should have.\n\nSnape was frightening when he knew everything in a room. There was something unexpectedly reassuring about hearing him say he did not.\n\n“Then we find out,” I said.\n\n“No.”\n\nI blinked.\n\n“No?”\n\n“You will find out from behind a shield.”\n\n“And you?”\n\n“I will handle the samples.”\n\n“You're still recovering from venom and nerve damage.”\n\n“I am also more familiar with Dark potions than you are.”\n\n“That does not make you poison-proof.”\n\n“It does make me less likely to kill us through ignorance.”\n\nI folded my arms.\n\nHe gave me a look that had probably terrorized adolescents for decades.\n\nIt did not improve with age.\n\n“No,” I said.\n\nHis voice dropped. “This is not a negotiation.”\n\n“Everything between adults is a negotiation unless someone is using force.”\n\nHis expression changed at that.\n\nI continued before he could decide whether to be angry.\n\n“We work together. You identify likely hazards. I handle precision work when your hand tires. We both stay behind the shield whenever the phials are open.”\n\n“I do not require assistance.”\n\n“You nearly dropped a memory two days ago.”\n\nHis face went white with anger.\n\nI regretted the cruelty immediately, but not the point.\n\n“I'm not saying you're incapable,” I said. “I'm saying your body is still healing, and pretending otherwise does not make you safer. It makes you easier to kill.”\n\nSilence.\n\nHis gaze went to the hidden compartment.\n\nWhen he spoke, the anger had become something colder.\n\n“I have been easy to kill for some time.”\n\n“No,” I said. “You've been willing to die. That's different.”\n\nHis eyes snapped to mine.\n\nThe words had come too fast to soften.\n\nI could have apologized.\n\nI did not.\n\nFor once, I wanted him to hear me without escape.\n\nHis throat worked.\n\n“Do not presume—”\n\n“I don't presume anything about why. I'm looking at the formula you wrote. Seven days of tissue saturation. Multiple neutralizers. A warning not to rely on it alone. You prepared to survive and then behaved as if survival was an administrative error.”\n\nHis face was unreadable.\n\nMine probably wasn't.\n\n“That is not professional analysis,” he said.\n\n“No.”\n\n“What is it?”\n\nI had no safe answer.\n\nConcern sounded too intimate.\n\nFrustration sounded too small.\n\n“I don't know yet,” I said.\n\nFor some reason, honesty unsettled him more than either would have.\n\nHe looked away first.\n\n“Shield,” he said.\n\nI exhaled.\n\n“Together?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\nIt was as close to surrender as Severus Snape seemed capable of giving.\n\nThe first of Alecto's phials contained a modified Befuddlement Draught with a pain catalyst. The second contained a corrosive suspension meant to scar without immediately disabling. The third was a concentration of something I had never seen outside restricted medical texts.\n\nSnape recognized it.\n\n“Stop.”\n\nI froze.\n\nHe leaned close to the glass, eyes narrowing.\n\n“Acromantula venom.”\n\n“Stabilized?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“With what?”\n\nHe stared.\n\nThen he smiled.\n\nIt was not a pleasant smile.\n\n“Poorly.”\n\nHe pointed at a faint sediment along the bottom.\n\n“The stabilizer is separating. Another month and this would have become inert.”\n\n“Deliberate?”\n\n“No. Incompetence.”\n\n“You sound delighted.”\n\n“I am capable of enjoying small mercies.”\n\n“Particularly when they insult a Carrow.”\n\n“Especially then.”\n\nWe documented everything.\n\nBy evening, we had enough evidence to prove that the hidden cache was not Snape's and that at least one potion had been altered recently.\n\nMcGonagall came down herself.\n\nHer face hardened as she read the report.\n\n“This goes to Kingsley tonight.”\n\nSnape's expression shuttered at the Minister's name.\n\n“He will require a statement,” she added.\n\n“I have given statements.”\n\n“You have given insults in the approximate shape of statements.”\n\n“They were accurate.”\n\nMcGonagall looked at me.\n\nI looked at the ceiling.\n\n“I shall send an Auror tomorrow,” she said.\n\nSnape's hand tightened on his cane.\n\n“No.”\n\n“Severus.”\n\n“No Aurors in my laboratory.”\n\n“It is not currently your laboratory.”\n\nThe words struck harder than she meant them to.\n\nI saw it.\n\nMcGonagall saw it too.\n\nHer mouth softened, but Snape had already turned away.\n\n“Use my office,” I said.\n\nBoth of them looked at me.\n\n“I don't have an office,” I clarified. “So we can invent one. Empty classroom, neutral ground, whatever makes everyone stop posturing long enough to exchange information.”\n\nMcGonagall's eyebrows rose.\n\nSnape looked murderous.\n\n“Tomorrow at ten,” she said.\n\nThen she left before either of us could object.\n\nI began packing instruments.\n\nSnape said, “You have an extraordinary talent for making my life worse.”\n\n“You were supposed to be dead. There's significant room for improvement.”\n\nHe stared at me.\n\nThen, impossibly, he laughed.\n\nIt hurt him. He pressed a hand to his throat and scowled as if the pain were my fault.\n\nI smiled anyway.\n\nOn the table between us, the blood-reactive potion snapped once more at the glass.\n\nThe past was not finished.\n\nNeither, apparently, was he.","word_count":2019},"after-always-20":{"number":20,"title":"A Door Left Open","subtitle":"After Always · Chapter 20 of 40","content":"For three days after the apology, Snape left his office door open.\n\nNot wide.\n\nNever wide.\n\nAbout six inches.\n\nEnough that I could see lamplight from the classroom.\n\nEnough that if I needed him, I did not have to knock on a closed barrier.\n\nHe did not mention it.\n\nNeither did I.\n\nWe returned to ordinary work.\n\nOrdinary, by then, meant arguing over aconite quality, drafting evidence summaries, practicing Patronus charms after dinner, and occasionally realizing we were standing too close.\n\nMy Patronus still refused to choose a recognizable form.\n\n“Again,” Snape said.\n\nSilver mist burst from my wand, gathered itself into something with legs, and collapsed.\n\nI swore.\n\n“Elegant.”\n\n“You're not helping.”\n\n“I am not responsible for your vocabulary.”\n\n“You're responsible for half of it.”\n\nHis mouth twitched.\n\n“Again.”\n\nI tried.\n\nThis time the shape ran three steps before dissolving.\n\nSnape's eyes brightened.\n\n“There.”\n\n“I saw.”\n\n“Then stop looking surprised.”\n\n“I am allowed to be pleased.”\n\n“Privately.”\n\n“Are you pleased?”\n\nHis expression went carefully blank.\n\nI smiled.\n\n“Thought so.”\n\nWe returned to the dungeon late.\n\nThe office door was open.\n\nSnape entered first and did not close it behind him.\n\nI sat at the classroom table with the evidence ledger while he sorted letters inside.\n\nFrom my chair, I could see only part of him: a sleeve, the edge of his desk, the corner of the hidden door.\n\nIt felt strangely domestic.\n\nNot romantic, exactly.\n\nMore intimate than that.\n\nThe comfort of knowing another person was nearby without needing their attention.\n\nI had almost finished the ledger when Snape called, “Come here.”\n\nI looked toward the doorway.\n\n“Permission?”\n\nA pause.\n\n“Yes.”\n\nI entered.\n\nHe had spread documents across the desk.\n\nAt the center was a letter from Harry Potter.\n\nSnape looked offended by its existence.\n\n“What does he want?” I asked.\n\n“To testify.”\n\n“I thought he already agreed.”\n\n“He has now written eight paragraphs explaining why.”\n\n“That sounds like Harry Potter.”\n\n“You know him?”\n\n“Only publicly.”\n\n“Then do not defend him.”\n\nI leaned over the letter.\n\nThe handwriting was messy and direct. I did not read the private details, only the lines Snape had marked.\n\nPotter intended to state that Snape had protected him, served Dumbledore, sent the doe Patronus, and remained loyal to the mission at the cost of his reputation and life.\n\nSnape had underlined reputation twice.\n\n“You hate this,” I said.\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Because it's wrong?”\n\n“No.”\n\n“Because it's Harry?”\n\n“Partly.”\n\n“Because he makes you sound heroic?”\n\nSnape's mouth tightened.\n\n“There was nothing heroic about many of my choices.”\n\n“He doesn't say there was.”\n\n“He will be heard as saying it.”\n\n“People do like simple stories.”\n\n“Yes.”\n\nI pulled the second chair closer and sat.\n\n“What do you want him to say?”\n\n“The truth.”\n\n“Which truth?”\n\nHis eyes narrowed.\n\nI gestured to the letter.\n\n“You've taught me better than that. Facts without context. Context without absolution. What do you want in the record?”\n\nSnape looked down at his hands.\n\n“That I joined willingly.”\n\nI stayed quiet.\n\n“That I served Voldemort before I turned.”\n\nI nodded.\n\n“That I passed on part of a prophecy without knowing whom it endangered, then cared who it endangered only when I learned Lily was a target.”\n\nHis voice was flat with practiced cruelty toward himself.\n\n“That my first request to Dumbledore was for her life, not her husband's or child's.”\n\n“Yes.”\n\nHis eyes flicked toward me, perhaps expecting interruption.\n\nI gave none.\n\n“That I bullied students. That I favored my own House. That I carried grudges into classrooms where they did not belong.”\n\nI nodded again.\n\n“And that you also spied for the Order for years,” I said when he stopped. “That you protected Harry. That you protected students under the Carrows. That you killed Dumbledore because he asked you to, not because Voldemort ordered you to.”\n\nHis face tightened.\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Both.”\n\n“Yes.”\n\nThe kettle clicked in the adjoining room, forgotten on its warmer. Severus watched the steam curl past the half-open door.\n\n\"You make a poor advocate,\" he said.\n\n\"Because I won't pretend you never hurt anyone?\"\n\n\"Because you insist on defending parts of my history even when it would be simpler to condemn the whole.\"\n\n\"That isn't a defense. It's a record.\"\n\nHe drew a sharp breath. Then he took the letter from the desk without opening it.\n\n\"Potter has no obligation to answer me,\" he said.\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"Nor do the students I treated badly.\"\n\n\"I know that too.\"\n\nHe studied me over the folded paper. \"You seem remarkably willing to live with incomplete outcomes.\"\n\n\"Not willing. Practicing.\"\n\nHe almost smiled. I touched the side of his hand and waited for him to decide whether it was welcome. His fingers turned beneath mine, but he did not reach for the letter again.\n\n\"I am ethically non-monogamous,\" I reminded him quietly, \"and I prefer polyamory. I don't need you to behave like the only person in my world. I do need you to be honest about what you can offer and what you still need to face.\"\n\n\"An exhausting philosophy.\"\n\n\"Only on days ending in Y.\"\n\nHe gave me a look that would have frightened a first-year into silence. I kissed his knuckles instead, after asking, and his expression became much less certain. The letter remained unanswered. That was his choice to make, not a test I could pass on his behalf.\n\nI looked at Potter's letter.\n\n“Then write back.”\n\n“Absolutely not.”\n\n“Coward.”\n\nHis head snapped up.\n\nThe word was dangerous with him.\n\nI knew why.\n\nI had used it anyway, lightly but not thoughtlessly.\n\nHis face went hard.\n\nThen he saw my expression.\n\nNot accusation.\n\nChallenge.\n\nA long silence.\n\n“Manipulative,” he said.\n\n“Effective.”\n\n“Cheap.”\n\n“Still effective.”\n\nHe took out a sheet of parchment.\n\nI stood.\n\n“I'll leave you to it.”\n\n“Stay.”\n\nThe word stopped me.\n\nSnape looked equally startled by it.\n\nI sat again.\n\nHe wrote.\n\nSlowly.\n\nThe letter was not sentimental.\n\nOf course it wasn't.\n\nHe thanked Potter for offering testimony, then requested that the testimony include facts less flattering to him. He listed them with brutal clarity. He stated that service after defection did not erase service before it. He asked Potter not to repeat private memories beyond what was legally necessary.\n\nThen he stopped.\n\n“What?” I asked.\n\n“Nothing.”\n\n“My favorite word.”\n\nHis mouth tightened.\n\nHe wrote one final line.\n\nI could not see it.\n\nHe folded the letter and sealed it.\n\n“What did you add?”\n\n“Private correspondence.”\n\n“Fair.”\n\nHe looked at me.\n\n“You are not going to ask?”\n\n“I just did.”\n\n“And accepted refusal.”\n\n“I'm growing.”\n\n“Disturbing.”\n\nThe sealed letter stayed on the desk while he straightened the rest of the paperwork. I kept thinking about the way he'd asked whether I intended to read it anyway.\n\n\"Can I ask a different question?\" I said.\n\nHe looked up over his glasses. \"You generally do.\"\n\n\"Do you want me here while you send it?\"\n\nHis expression changed, just a little. \"I had not considered that it was something I could request.\"\n\n\"You can request company without handing me the envelope.\"\n\nHe examined the wax seal. Whatever he'd written about the past was his to tell. I knew enough about the people involved to understand how dangerous a careless rumor could be, and not enough to decide the details for them.\n\n\"I'd like you to stay,\" he said.\n\n\"All right.\"\n\nI sat with my notes while he recorded the recipient's address on a separate slip. He didn't show me the letter. I didn't pretend not to notice that he was trembling when he put it into the outgoing tray.\n\n\"Was it difficult?\" I asked.\n\n\"Appallingly.\"\n\n\"Want me to tell you that you've done a good thing?\"\n\n\"I should prefer you tell me whether the seal is intact.\"\n\n\"It is.\"\n\n\"Then we shall begin with that.\"\n\nHe found a second envelope for the school records and placed the private letter in the correct tray. It was not a ceremony, only an act of putting one piece of information where it belonged.\n\nI leaned back in the chair. \"For what it's worth, I like that you can say no to me.\"\n\n\"An unfortunate revelation.\"\n\n\"I'm ethically non-monogamous, and I prefer polyamory. Boundaries aren't a test of who loves someone best. They're part of how I know I can keep choosing a relationship.\"\n\nHe went quiet. Then he shifted the candle farther from the paper, an almost absurdly ordinary gesture.\n\n\"I understand the argument,\" he said. \"I may even be persuaded by it.\"\n\nI smiled. We sat together until the ink dried, and I went back to my own work without opening the letter or imagining that I had earned the right to.\n\nWe returned to the classroom.\n\nNear midnight, I packed my notes.\n\nSnape remained in the office.\n\nThe door was still open.\n\n“Good night,” I called.\n\nHe appeared in the doorway.\n\n“Good night.”\n\nI took three steps.\n\n“Wait.”\n\nI turned.\n\nHe stood with one hand on the doorframe.\n\n“I wrote that I remember his mother as herself,” he said.\n\nI went still.\n\nNot just Lily as Harry's mother.\n\nLily as herself.\n\n“And?”\n\n“That he should do the same.”\n\nSomething tightened behind my ribs.\n\n“That was kind.”\n\nHis face immediately closed.\n\n“Do not ruin it.”\n\n“I wouldn't dream of it.”\n\nHe looked at me.\n\nI looked back.\n\nFor a moment, neither of us moved.\n\nThen he stepped aside from the doorway.\n\nNot away.\n\nAside.\n\nMaking room.\n\n“You may use the kettle,” he said.\n\nI blinked.\n\n“In the back room?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Without asking?”\n\n“Within reason.”\n\nI smiled slowly.\n\n“Careful.”\n\nHis eyes narrowed.\n\n“That sounds dangerously like trust.”\n\n“Go to bed.”\n\nI laughed and left.\n\nThe open door did not remain open every night.\n\nThat would have made it a performance.\n\nSometimes I arrived and found it shut because Severus wanted quiet. Sometimes the ward flashed red because he was speaking with Minerva or reviewing testimony for the Ministry. Once I knocked and he simply called, “Not tonight.”\n\nI went upstairs.\n\nThe next morning he stopped beside me at breakfast.\n\n“You are not offended?”\n\n“By a closed door?”\n\nHis expression said that yes, apparently this was a serious question.\n\n“No.”\n\n“You did not ask why.”\n\n“You told me what you needed.”\n\nHe studied me with the wary concentration he normally reserved for unknown reagents.\n\n“That is all?”\n\n“That is all.”\n\nHe sat across from me.\n\nFive minutes later, without explanation, he pushed the marmalade toward my side of the table because he had remembered I liked it.\n\nI did not make a fuss.\n\nThe open door mattered because it was chosen.\n\nSo did the closed one.\n\nBy the end of the week, I understood that intimacy with Severus was not constant access. It was knowing a boundary would survive being used.\n\nBehind me, he did not close the office door.","word_count":1798},"after-always-33":{"number":33,"title":"The First Morning","subtitle":"After Always · Chapter 33 of 40","content":"The first morning I woke beside Severus Snape, he was already awake.\n\nOf course he was.\n\nHe lay on his back staring at the ceiling with the expression of a man conducting a hostile review of existence.\n\nI blinked at him.\n\n“Good morning.”\n\nHis eyes moved toward me.\n\n“Debatable.”\n\nI smiled into the pillow.\n\nWe had spent the night at Spinner's End.\n\nNot accidentally.\n\nNot because a storm trapped us or the Floo failed or some emergency made privacy convenient.\n\nWe had chosen it.\n\nThat distinction mattered enough for Snape to ask once more if I was comfortable. I said yes, and he closed the bedroom door.\n\nWe kissed, talked through the pauses, and laughed when we both reached for the teapot at once. When we were ready to be alone, the rest of the night belonged to us.\n\nWe had gone slowly.\n\nWe had stopped once.\n\nWe had started again because we both wanted to.\n\nNow morning made everything ordinary in the most alarming way.\n\nHis hair was everywhere.\n\nOne side had flattened against the pillow. The other stood at an angle I had never seen in public.\n\nI stared.\n\n“No,” he said.\n\nI laughed.\n\n“You look—”\n\n“Finish that sentence carefully.”\n\n“Human.”\n\nHis eyes narrowed.\n\n“Disappointing, I know.”\n\n“Profoundly.”\n\nI moved closer.\n\nHe went still for the smallest second before relaxing.\n\nMorning touch, I realized, was new territory.\n\nLast night every contact had been asked in explicit language.\n\nNow I let my hand rest on the blanket between us.\n\n“Can I?”\n\nHis gaze dropped to it.\n\n“Yes.”\n\nI touched his shoulder.\n\nThen his chest.\n\nNothing sexual.\n\nJust warm skin and the rhythm of his breathing beneath my palm.\n\nHis hand covered mine.\n\n“This is strange,” he said.\n\n“What is?”\n\n“You being here.”\n\n“Bad strange?”\n\nHe considered.\n\n“No.”\n\n“Good strange?”\n\n“Do not make me use that phrase.”\n\n“Neutral strange?”\n\n“Unfamiliar.”\n\nThat was more honest.\n\nI nodded.\n\n“Me too.”\n\nHis eyebrows rose.\n\n“What?”\n\n“You appear comfortable.”\n\n“I am performing competence.”\n\nHis mouth curved.\n\n“Recognizable technique.”\n\nIn the morning, we stayed beneath the blankets longer than either of us usually allowed ourselves, then made tea before facing the day.\n\nSnape pretended this was because the room was cold.\n\nI pretended to believe him.\n\nEventually the kettle downstairs whistled on a timing charm.\n\nHe closed his eyes.\n\n“You enchanted the kettle?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“For what time?”\n\n“Seven.”\n\nI looked at the clock.\n\n“It is seven.”\n\n“I am aware.”\n\n“You planned morning tea.”\n\n“I plan most things.”\n\n“Romantic.”\n\nHe pulled the blanket over his face.\n\nI laughed so hard I had to sit up.\n\n“This relationship is intolerable,” he said from beneath the blanket.\n\n“You're hiding.”\n\n“I am reconsidering.”\n\n“Agreement two. No disappearing as strategy.”\n\nThe blanket lowered.\n\nHis eyes were amused.\n\n“I hate that document.”\n\n“You wrote half of it.”\n\n“An error.”\n\nWe went downstairs.\n\nThe kitchen was cold, but sunlight reached the table. Snape brewed tea. I found bread and eggs. He objected to my method of cooking eggs.\n\n“You are stirring too aggressively.”\n\n“They are eggs.”\n\n“Texture remains relevant.”\n\n“You're not allowed to turn breakfast into Potions.”\n\n“Everything involving heat, timing, and ingredients has standards.”\n\n“Should I use a silver cauldron?”\n\n“Do not be ridiculous. Copper would distribute heat better.”\n\nI stared.\n\nHe stared back.\n\nThen we both started laughing.\n\nIt was absurdly domestic.\n\nThat frightened me a little.\n\nI realized it while buttering toast.\n\nThe feeling must have crossed my face because Snape stopped criticizing the eggs.\n\n“What?”\n\n“Nothing.”\n\nHis eyebrow rose.\n\nI sighed.\n\n“This is nice.”\n\nHis expression became wary.\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“And nice can become expected.”\n\nHe waited.\n\n“And expected can become required.”\n\nUnderstanding moved through his face.\n\n“You are afraid of obligation.”\n\n“Sometimes.”\n\n“Of me?”\n\n“Not exactly.”\n\nHe set down his cup.\n\n“Explain exactly.”\n\nI smiled faintly.\n\n“You really hate ambiguity.”\n\n“Yes.”\n\nI leaned against the counter.\n\n“I don't want waking up here to become something either of us has to do to prove we're serious.”\n\nSnape considered.\n\n“No.”\n\n“No?”\n\n“It will not.”\n\n“That simple?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\nHe picked up his cup.\n\nI stared.\n\n“You're good at this sometimes.”\n\n“Do not spread the rumor.”\n\nWarmth returned.\n\nI put the eggs on plates.\n\nSnape tasted them.\n\n“Well?”\n\nHe chewed with maddening deliberation.\n\n“Acceptable.”\n\nI threw a napkin at him.\n\nHe caught it.\n\nSeverus set the captured napkin neatly beside his plate, as if it were evidence of a particularly appalling breach of etiquette.\n\n\"I assume you're going to tell somebody about this,\" he said.\n\n\"Your mastery of breakfast?\"\n\n\"The hair.\"\n\n\"Ah. The classified hair.\"\n\nHe gave me a poisonous look. I caught myself smiling at the way the sunlight found the softer edges of his face when he wasn't wearing a teaching expression.\n\n\"I have plans at the archive tomorrow,\" I said. \"I'll be leaving early.\"\n\nHis fork stopped.\n\n\"I didn't want you to discover it when I was already gone,\" I added. \"I've told you that I practice ethical non-monogamy and prefer polyamory. It doesn't mean I owe you every hour, but it also doesn't mean I want to surprise you with plans you've had no chance to discuss.\"\n\n\"Have I implied you must apply for permission?\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"Good.\" He took a sip of tea. \"I would despise being put in charge of your calendar.\"\n\n\"You've reorganized it twice.\"\n\n\"Because it is illegible.\"\n\nI leaned across the table. \"Can I kiss you before I go?\"\n\n\"Your eggs are getting cold.\"\n\n\"That's not an answer.\"\n\nHe lowered the cup. \"Yes.\"\n\nThe kiss was brief, then less brief when he caught the edge of my sleeve. We separated because we had things to do, not because tenderness had run out. His napkin remained perfectly folded. He had loosened one button of his collar while I wasn't looking, and I pretended I hadn't noticed.\n\nAfter breakfast, we dressed.\n\nSnape's shirt collar caught against the scar on his throat. He hissed and pulled it away.\n\nI watched his hand tremble.\n\n“Help?” I asked.\n\nA pause.\n\n“Yes.”\n\nI stepped close.\n\nHe stood still while I fastened the top buttons more loosely and folded the collar so it did not rub.\n\nMy fingers moved near the scar without touching it.\n\nHis breathing stayed even.\n\n“Better?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\nI smoothed the fabric once over his shoulder.\n\nHe looked at me.\n\n“You are staring.”\n\n“I like you.”\n\n“That is not a defense.”\n\n“I love you.”\n\nHis face immediately betrayed him.\n\nColor.\n\nSoftness.\n\nAnnoyance at both.\n\n“That is even less relevant.”\n\n“I disagree.”\n\nHe kissed my forehead.\n\nQuickly, as if hoping to escape consequences.\n\nI caught his coat before he could step away.\n\n“Again.”\n\nHis eyebrow rose.\n\n“Demanding.”\n\n“Yes.”\n\nHe kissed me properly.\n\nWhen we finally left the house, he locked the door and stood on the pavement beside me.\n\n“Tonight?” he asked.\n\nNot an expectation.\n\nA question.\n\nI thought about my own room at Hogwarts, my work, the pleasure of missing someone by choice rather than obligation.\n\n“Tomorrow,” I said.\n\nSnape nodded.\n\n“Tomorrow.”\n\nNo hurt.\n\nNo punishment.\n\nJust a plan.\n\nThe first morning did not turn us into one person.\n\nIt gave us breakfast, bad hair, careful questions, and a door we could leave separately.\n\nFor me, that was more romantic than destiny.\n\nWe ate breakfast at the small table by the window.\n\nSnape had toast. I had tea strong enough to qualify as disciplinary action. Neither of us commented on the fact that he had remembered exactly how I took it.\n\nFor several minutes, the most intimate thing in the room was butter.\n\nI liked that.\n\nIt gave the night before somewhere ordinary to land.\n\nSnape opened the newspaper, read two paragraphs, and lowered it again.\n\n“You are watching me.”\n\n“You're holding the paper upside down.”\n\nHe looked at it.\n\nTurned it around.\n\n“I was testing the print quality.”\n\n“Obviously.”\n\n“Do not become smug before nine.”\n\n“What time is smugness permitted?”\n\n“Never. I was attempting compromise.”\n\nI laughed, and some of the remaining tension left his shoulders.\n\nThat was when I understood that he was waiting for the bill.\n\nNot money. Meaning. Expectation. The invisible invoice people sometimes handed each other after vulnerability: I gave you this, therefore you owe me that.\n\nI set my cup down.\n\n“Nothing changed that I didn't agree to change.”\n\nHis eyes lifted.\n\n“Explain.”\n\n“You don't owe me more access because we were close last night. I don't get to decide we're inseparable because I woke up here. You still get doors. I still get mine.”\n\nHis face went very still.\n\n“And if I want you here again?”\n\n“Ask.”\n\n“That simple.”\n\n“Yes.”\n\nHe looked irritated by the answer, which meant it had probably reached something important.\n\n“And if you do not?” he asked.\n\n“I'll say no.”\n\nHis mouth tightened.\n\n“Not because I don't care about you,” I added. “Because I want yes to mean yes when I say it.”\n\nA long silence.\n\nThen Snape nodded.\n\n“Reasonable.”\n\n“Write down the date.”\n\n“Do not ruin it.”\n\nI grinned.\n\nWe finished breakfast.\n\nAfterward, Snape gathered the plates before I could. He carried them to the sink with the stiff concentration of a man who had survived a war and remained suspicious of domestic labor.\n\n“You can use magic,” I said.\n\n“I am aware.”\n\n“You are washing a plate by hand.”\n\n“The spell left residue yesterday.”\n\n“That is the most Severus Snape explanation possible.”\n\nHe ignored me.\n\nI dried the plate he handed over.\n\nThat became the shape of the morning: nothing dramatic, everything slightly new. He made a list for the laboratory. I corrected his list because he had forgotten food. He added coffee under protest. I added a second line that said ACTUAL FOOD. He crossed it out. I wrote it again.\n\nBy ten we were arguing about a sleeping draught formula that had been used on injured veterans because it suppressed dreams too aggressively.\n\n“The memories return on waking with increased disorientation,” I said.\n\n“I know.”\n\n“Then why are we keeping the standard concentration?”\n\n“We are not.”\n\nI looked at him.\n\nSnape slid a notebook across the table. The page contained three alternate ratios and a list of possible stabilizers.\n\n“You've already been working on it.”\n\n“For six weeks.”\n\n“And you were going to tell me when?”\n\n“When you stopped asking irritating questions.”\n\n“So never.”\n\n“Precisely.”\n\nI read the notes again.\n\nThe problem was difficult. Not impossible. More importantly, it mattered to him for reasons he would not say aloud.\n\nWar had left too many people unable to sleep.\n\nSnape knew something about that.\n\nI closed the notebook. “All right.”\n\n“All right what?”\n\n“Let's make something better.”\n\nHis expression shifted.\n\nNot softer exactly.\n\nLess alone.\n\n“After breakfast?” he asked dryly.\n\nI held up the clean plate. “Apparently we're very domestic now.”\n\n“God help us.”\n\nThe laboratory waited downstairs.\n\nSo did the problem he could not solve alone.","word_count":1793},"after-always-47":{"number":47,"title":"A Teacher, Not a Verdict","subtitle":"The Lanternweald — fourth expansion pass","content":"The apprentice arrived carrying a notebook filled with Severus Snape's heroic deeds.\n\nThat was not what they'd intended, of course. They believed they had compiled a respectful account of a complicated man. The fact that it sounded like an advertisement for sainthood had escaped them.\n\n\"My name is Orin,\" they said, offering a hand. \"I'm studying historical testimony for the Moonroot oral-history program.\"\n\nSeverus looked at the notebook.\n\n\"How unfortunate.\"\n\nOrin smiled nervously.\n\n\"I've read about your work during the war.\"\n\n\"Have you?\"\n\n\"Yes. I think you were one of the greatest heroes of your generation.\"\n\nThe silence was extraordinary.\n\nI was arranging copies of the Closed Bells report on the shelf. I stopped moving. From the corner of my eye, I saw Severus's expression settle into something very still.\n\n\"On what evidence?\" he asked.\n\nOrin opened the notebook.\n\n\"Well, you were a double agent. You protected students. You made sacrifices no one understood until afterward.\"\n\n\"And?\"\n\n\"And you were misunderstood.\"\n\n\"Was I?\"\n\nOrin looked at me for help.\n\nI set down the papers.\n\n\"Why don't you tell him which sources you used?\"\n\nThey listed several popular histories, an article written shortly after the war, and a memoir by somebody who had met Severus twice. None of the material was wholly invented. Some of it was incomplete. All of it treated the ending as though it retroactively explained every choice that came before.\n\nSeverus took the notebook, with permission, and turned to the first page.\n\n\"Here you say I had no choice.\"\n\nOrin nodded.\n\n\"That was the tragic part.\"\n\n\"No,\" he said. \"That is the part you have mistaken.\"\n\nThe apprentice looked wounded.\n\nSeverus returned the notebook.\n\n\"I made choices. Some were constrained by threats. Some were made in ignorance. Others were wrong. If you remove my agency, you do not make me admirable. You make the harm inexplicable.\"\n\nOrin swallowed.\n\n\"I didn't mean to insult you.\"\n\n\"You have not insulted me. You have made a bad argument.\"\n\nI bit back a smile.\n\nIt wasn't a gentle conversation. But Severus was teaching, not sentencing. I could see him making an effort to leave the apprentice room to correct themself.\n\n\"Would you show me where it goes wrong?\" Orin asked.\n\nSeverus looked surprised.\n\nThen he pulled out a chair.\n\n\"Sit.\"\n\nFor the next hour, he took the notebook apart line by line.\n\nHe asked which claims had witnesses. Which were based on later interpretation. Which events had harmed people, regardless of his reasons. Where Orin had substituted an adjective for an explanation.\n\n\"Brave is not a substitute for precise,\" he said.\n\nOrin wrote that down.\n\nThey worked without an audience until Neri came through the doorway to retrieve the ledger report. Neri paused, listened, and quietly continued to the other room.\n\nI brought tea.\n\nSeverus accepted his cup without breaking the sentence he was explaining.\n\nOrin drank theirs too quickly and burned their tongue.\n\n\"Careful,\" I said.\n\nThey winced.\n\nSeverus looked at the cup.\n\n\"An instructive example of consequences arriving before understanding.\"\n\nI laughed.\n\nOrin laughed too, after a moment.\n\nBy afternoon, the notebook had more corrections than original lines. Orin looked exhausted.\n\n\"Do you still think I should write the piece?\" they asked.\n\n\"That depends on whether you intend to revise it.\"\n\n\"I do.\"\n\n\"Then write it.\"\n\n\"What should I call you?\"\n\nSeverus considered.\n\n\"Someone who made choices and must answer for them.\"\n\n\"That's a terrible title.\"\n\n\"An honest one.\"\n\nI watched Orin think.\n\n\"Maybe that's the point,\" they said.\n\nSeverus gave the smallest nod.\n\nBefore Orin went, Neri returned with a sealed paper sleeve.\n\n\"One of your references contains testimony given confidentially,\" they said. \"You may document that a statement was received. You may not publish its contents.\"\n\nOrin frowned. \"But I need the detail to support my argument.\"\n\n\"Then you require a different argument,\" Severus said.\n\n\"How can I tell the truth if I leave things out?\"\n\n\"By declining to steal someone else's truth.\"\n\nThe words landed harder than either of us expected. Severus stared at the papers in his hands. He had spent a lifetime knowing what secrets could cost, and he was trying not to turn that knowledge into another weapon.\n\nOrin considered the sleeve. \"Could I ask the witness?\"\n\n\"If they can be approached without identifying them,\" Neri said. \"And if you are ready to hear no.\"\n\nOrin pulled the confidential quotation from the draft. We found two publicly available accounts that supported part of the chronology but not the detail they wanted to claim. Orin wrote UNKNOWN beside the unresolved date.\n\n\"People will think I didn't do my research,\" they said.\n\n\"Better that than pretending the evidence is complete,\" Severus answered.\n\nI watched him turn the notebook toward the apprentice rather than reaching across to correct it himself.\n\n\"Your work must be yours,\" he said. \"So must your mistakes.\"\n\nOrin made the change in their own handwriting. Their paper was less flattering, much more difficult, and finally honest.\n\nOrin sat a little straighter when Neri brought over the public catalogue. They began comparing each citation against the original entry instead of copying a celebrated historian's paraphrase.\n\n\"This one has the date wrong,\" they said.\n\nSeverus examined the reference. \"By six months.\"\n\n\"That changes whether the witness could have seen the event.\"\n\n\"It does.\"\n\nOrin struck out the paragraph without looking to see whether he approved. I could tell it cost them something. The deleted sentence had been the loveliest in the essay, and it was now useless.\n\n\"Do you want help rewriting it?\" I asked.\n\n\"No,\" they said. \"I want you to check whether my replacement makes a claim I haven't proved.\"\n\nSeverus considered them for a long moment. \"An improvement.\"\n\nNeri set down a second ledger with a restricted-material notice still attached. Nobody moved to open it. I loved that the quiet little decision could go uncelebrated.\n\nLater, as we walked out of Moonroot Archive, I reached for Severus's hand. He glanced down as though he might object to the public display.\n\n\"You needn't,\" I said.\n\n\"I am aware.\"\n\nHis fingers closed around mine, cool from the reading room. \"You are entirely too pleased with yourself.\"\n\n\"You're holding my hand.\"\n\n\"A correct observation.\"\n\n\"Was that a compliment?\"\n\n\"It was not.\"\n\nI smiled at the stone path ahead of us. His sharpness had not softened into somebody else's gentle voice. He had simply decided that, for the next few steps, I was welcome beside him.\n\nWhen the apprentice left, they took their notebook and a stack of primary-source references. No blessing. No grand declaration. Just work to do.\n\nI was proud of Severus, but I waited until the archive was nearly empty before telling him.\n\n\"You didn't let them turn you into a monument,\" I said.\n\n\"I have never cared for statues.\"\n\n\"Toph would approve.\"\n\n\"That is a deeply unsettling endorsement.\"\n\nI laughed.\n\nSeverus gathered the loose papers into a careful stack.\n\n\"Orin will probably write something foolish anyway,\" he said.\n\n\"Maybe. But they asked to learn. You gave them a way.\"\n\nHe looked down at his hands.\n\n\"I used to think teaching was the art of making students fear mistakes.\"\n\n\"And now?\"\n\n\"Now I suspect it is the art of making them capable of recognizing one.\"\n\nI stepped closer.\n\n\"That sounds suspiciously kind.\"\n\n\"Do not spread the rumor.\"\n\nThe evening light caught the edge of his hair. There were dark smudges of ink on his fingers, and he had forgotten to remove his outer robe after the morning's catalog work. I brushed a speck of dust from his shoulder.\n\nHe watched me.\n\n\"You're staring.\"\n\n\"Am I?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"I like you.\"\n\n\"An alarming lapse of judgment.\"\n\n\"I've been making it for some time.\"\n\nHe tried to look severe. His mouth betrayed him.\n\nI took his hand and led him toward the little reading nook behind the archive's west room. It was empty, quiet, and warm enough to escape the draft from the main hall.\n\nSeverus stopped beside the shelf.\n\n\"Jasper.\"\n\n\"Yes?\"\n\n\"I had expected you to find the discussion tiresome.\"\n\n\"I found it difficult. That's not the same.\"\n\nHe looked at me.\n\n\"I know you have many people you care about, and I know we have agreed what that means for us. Sometimes I still wonder whether you'd rather spend your evenings with someone less complicated.\"\n\nI felt a small ache in my chest.\n\n\"Severus, you're not a problem I agreed to solve. You're someone I choose. That doesn't require you to become easy.\"\n\nHe let out a slow breath.\n\n\"And if I remain profoundly unpleasant?\"\n\n\"Then I'll tell you when you're being unpleasant.\"\n\n\"How romantic.\"\n\n\"Would you like a demonstration?\"\n\nHis eyebrows rose.\n\nI kissed him before he could find a better insult. He answered with a warmth that made me forget the smell of old paper for a moment. His fingers settled against my waist, asking without words whether I wanted him closer.\n\nI did.\n\nOrin brought the second draft to the desk before the archive closed. At the back was a list of names gathered from Severus's old school records.\n\n“I thought I should interview them,” Orin said. “If the history needs more than one voice.”\n\nSeverus read the list without touching it. “Have any of these people asked to participate?”\n\n“No.”\n\n“Then you have compiled a set of people to be bothered about the worst years of their lives. A remarkable feat of scholarship.”\n\nOrin flushed. I touched the corner of the paper, not to rescue them, but to keep the discussion from becoming another public humiliation.\n\n“Some might want to speak,” I said. “They should get an invitation that lets them say no without explaining why.”\n\nNeri came over with the archive's standard contact form. It gave potential witnesses control over whether to speak, how to be quoted and what records they wanted kept closed. A blank reply was not consent. There would be no repeated letters after a refusal.\n\nOrin read it twice. “I was trying to make the record fair.”\n\n“Then begin,” Severus said, “by not assigning your research subjects a duty to make it fair for you.”\n\nThey struck several names from the preliminary interview list, including the people whose current addresses had been obtained from private correspondence. Severus checked the final form for ambiguous wording, then put his pen down.\n\n“I cannot certify the quality of what people will tell you,” he said. “I can only tell you where my own account is incomplete.”\n\nOrin nodded, older in that moment than when they'd arrived with a notebook full of praise. The archive would not finish the story on schedule. It would give the people named in it a choice.\n\nWe stayed until the last lamp in the archive was turned down, then went home together. The evening was private and unhurried, the kind of intimacy that needed no witness or verdict.\n\nThe next morning, a note from Orin arrived at Moonroot.\n\nThey had rewritten the opening.\n\nIt began, not with an accusation or a hymn of praise, but with a question about the difference between what a person intended and what their choices did.\n\nSeverus read it and set it on the table.\n\n\"Better,\" he said.\n\nI smiled.\n\nFor him, that was practically applause.","word_count":1869}};var s=lib.series.find(x=>x.series_slug==="after-always");if(!s)return;for(var i=0;i<s.chapters.length;i++){var c=s.chapters[i],e=edits[c.id];if(e)Object.assign(c,e);}s.total_word_count=79662;})();
